As we will show, this algorithm is correct under C20 \emph{provided that the following two properties hold}:
\begin{eqnarray}
\textit{All accesses of the counter are either increments or release decrements.}\label{eq:arc-accesses}\\
\textit{No access of the counter reads a value $v \le 0$.}\label{eq:arc-no-zero}
\end{eqnarray}
(In particular, clearly, the presence of relaxed decrements would break the algorithm.) Verifying (\ref{eq:arc-accesses}) or (\ref{eq:arc-no-zero}) using separation logic is tricky, and we don't know how to do it. Indeed, consider LBf above. Suppose that we want to verify that all modifications of $X$ and $Y$ are release writes, so that they synchronize with the acquire fences. The problem here is that both writes are \emph{behind} fences; to reason about the fences, we would have to assume the property before verifying it, which is unsound.
Instead, we simply assume these properties. Actually, (\ref{eq:arc-accesses}) can be verified using some \emph{syntactic} approach, such as using a Java-like static type system to syntactically check all accesses of the $\mathsf{Arc.counter}$ field. For (\ref{eq:arc-no-zero}), in contrast, we have no suggestions; we leave it as an open problem.

In \S\ref{sec:c20-logic}, we propose an approach for verifying programs under C20, under certain assumptions to be checked by other means, and we use it to verify Core ARC. Specifically, we propose an operational semantics (opsem) for C20 programs instrumented with an \emph{atomic specification} for each location accessed atomically, that specifies a set of \emph{enabled operations} for the location, as well as a \emph{tied precondition} for each enabled operation and a \emph{tied postcondition} for each enabled operation-result pair, both elements of an algebra of \emph{tied resources}. We assume all access events that occur are enabled. In the opsem, an atomic access nondeterministically yields any result enabled under its atomic specification. We argue the opsem's soundness with respect to C20's axiomatic semantics, and we apply the Iris \cite{iris,iris-groundup} logic to the opsem to verify Core ARC.

In \S\ref{sec:xc20-logic}, then, we adapt this approach to obtain an approach for verifying programs under XC20, without any assumptions, and we again use it to verify Core ARC. We use the grounding guarantees offered by XC20 to prove that all atomic accesses of a location are enabled under its atomic specification. We finish by discussing related work (\S\ref{sec:related-work}) and offering a conclusion (\S\ref{sec:conclusion}).

\section{A verification approach for C20}\label{sec:c20-logic}

In this section, we propose an operational semantics for C20 (\S\ref{sec:c20-opsem}) and we apply it to verify Core ARC (\S\ref{sec:arc-c20}).

\subsection{An operational semantics for C20}\label{sec:c20-opsem}

We define an interleaving operational semantics for an instrumented version of our programming language.

\subsubsection{Instrumented programs}

We add two auxiliary commands to the syntax of the programming language: $\mathbf{begin\_atomic}(\ell, \Sigma)$ and
$\mathbf{end\_atomic}(\ell)$.
When turning a nonatomic memory location $\ell$ into an atomic location using the $\mathbf{begin\_atomic}$ command,
one has to specify an \emph{atomic specification} $\Sigma = (v_0, \mathcal{R}_\mathsf{G}, \mathcal{R}_\mathsf{L}, \rho_0, \mathsf{pre}, \mathsf{post})$ consisting of an initial value $v_0$, 
cancellative commutative monoids\footnote{A monoid is a set $R$ with an associative binary \emph{composition} operator $\cdot$ and a unit element $0$. It is \emph{cancellative} if $v \cdot v_0 = v' \cdot v_0 \Rightarrow v = v'$.} of global tied resources $\mathcal{R}_\mathsf{G}$ (ranged over by $\rho$) and local tied resources $\mathcal{R}_\mathsf{L}$ (ranged over by $\theta$), an initial global tied resource $\rho_0 \in \mathcal{R}_\mathsf{G}$, a partial function $\mathsf{pre} : \mathcal{O} \rightharpoonup \mathcal{R}_\mathsf{G} \times \mathcal{R}_\mathsf{L}$, where the set of \emph{operations} $\mathcal{O} = \{\mathsf{R}_\mathsf{rlx}, \mathsf{R}_\mathsf{acq}, \mathsf{fence}_\mathsf{acq}, \mathsf{fence}_\mathsf{rel}\} \cup \{\mathsf{W}_\mathsf{rlx}(v), \mathsf{W}_\mathsf{rel}(v)\ |\ v \in \mathbb{Z}\} \cup \{\mathsf{RMW}_\mathsf{rlx}(f), \mathsf{RMW}_\mathsf{rel}(f),$ $\mathsf{RMW}_\mathsf{acq}(f), \mathsf{RMW}_\mathsf{acqrel}(f)\ |\ f \in \mathbb{Z} \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}\}$, mapping each \emph{enabled operation} $o$ to its \emph{tied precondition} $(\rho, \theta)$ consisting of a \emph{global tied precondition} $\rho$ and a \emph{local tied precondition} $\theta$, and a partial function $\mathsf{post} : \mathcal{O} \times \mathbb{Z} \rightharpoonup \mathcal{R}_\mathsf{G} \times \mathcal{R}_\mathsf{L}$ mapping a pair of an enabled operation $o$ and a result value $v$ at which $o$ is \emph{enabled} to a \emph{tied postcondition} $(\rho', \theta')$ consisting of a \emph{global tied postcondition} $\rho'$ and a \emph{local tied postcondition} $\theta'$.

For now, we assume that for any $\mathbf{begin\_atomic}(\ell, \Sigma)$ event, all accesses of $\ell$ that do not happen before it happen after it and are atomic operation-result pairs that are enabled under $\Sigma$, i.e.~pairs $(o, v) \in \mathrm{dom}\,\mathsf{post}$.\footnote{This implies the program has no $\mathbf{end\_atomic}(\ell)$ operations, and also does not deallocate the location, suggesting a garbage-collected setting. Note: other resources, like the ARC's payload, might still be non-garbage-collected native resources.} In \S\ref{sec:xc20-logic}, we eliminate all of these assumptions.

The syntax of instrumented programs is as follows:
$$\begin{array}{r l}
e ::= & v\ |\ x\ |\ e + e\ |\ e = e\\
c ::= & \mathbf{cons}(\overline{e})\ |\ [e]_\mathbf{na}\ |\ [e] :=_\mathbf{na} e\ |\ \mathbf{free}(e)\\
& |\ \auxcode{\mathbf{begin\_atomic}(e, \Sigma)}\ |\ \auxcode{\mathbf{end\_atomic}(e)}\ |\ o(e)\\
& |\ \mathbf{if}\ e\ \mathbf{then}\ c\ |\ \mathbf{let}\ x = c\ \mathbf{in}\ c\ |\ \mathbf{fork}(c)
\end{array}$$
Command $o(e)$ applies memory access operation $o$ to the location yielded by $e$. Notice that even fence operations are qualified by a location this way. Indeed, our operational semantics takes into account only the synchronization induced by the interplay between the fence and the accesses of this particular location. It is future work to investigate the severity of this incompleteness and to lift it, if necessary.

\subsubsection{Consistency of a tied resource with an event}

We derive a cancellative commutative monoid of \emph{thread-bound tied resources} $\mathcal{R}_\mathsf{B} = \mathit{ThreadIds} \rightarrow \mathcal{R}_\mathsf{L}$, ranged over by $\Theta$. Each element of $\mathcal{R}_\mathsf{B}$ associates a local tied resource with each thread. Its composition is the pointwise composition and its unit element is the function that maps all thread ids to 0. We further derive a cancellative commutative monoid of \emph{total tied resources} $\mathcal{R}_\mathsf{T} = \mathcal{R}_\mathsf{G} \times \mathcal{R}_\mathsf{B}$ (ranged over by $\omega$), whose composition is the componentwise composition and whose unit element is $(0, 0)$. We will abuse $\cdot$ and $0$ to denote the composition and unit element of any of these monoids. Initially, there are no local tied resources, so the initial total tied resource is $(\rho_0, 0)$.

An \emph{event labelling function} $\mathsf{lab} : E \rightarrow \mathsf{LAB}$ with $\mathsf{LAB} = \mathit{ThreadIds} \times \mathbb{Z} \times \mathbb{Z} \times (\mathcal{O} \cup \{\mathsf{init}\})$ maps an \emph{event} $e \in E$ to a \emph{label} $(t, \ell, v, o)$ that associates the event with its thread $t$, the location $\ell$ operated on, the result value $v$ of the operation, and the operation $o$ (or the special initialization event $\mathsf{init}$). The result of a write or fence is always 0; the result of a read-modify-write (RMW) is the value that was read, before the modification.

An \emph{atomic location trace} $(E, \mathsf{lab}, \mo, \rf, \hb)$ for a location $\ell$ under an atomic specification $\Sigma$ consists of a set of events $E$, a labeling function $\mathsf{lab} : E \rightarrow \mathsf{LAB}$, a \emph{modification order} $\mo \subseteq E \times E$, a \emph{reads-from} relation $\rf \subseteq E \times E$, and a \emph{happens-before} relation $\hb \subseteq E \times E$, where:
\begin{itemize}
\item All events operate on location $\ell$
\item Exactly one event is an $\mathsf{init}$ event, which writes $\Sigma.v_0$ and is related by $\hb$ to all other events
\item $\mo$ totally orders the modification events (the $\mathsf{init}$ events, the write events and the RMW events)
\item The trace is $\rf$-complete: for each read or RMW event $e\in E$, there exists a write, RMW, or $\mathsf{init}$ event $e' \in E$ such that $(e', e) \in \rf$.
\item $\rf$ relates each modification event to the read and RMW events that read from it
\item Each RMW event reads from the modification that immediately precedes it in $\mo$
\item The C20 coherence axioms hold
\item $\hb$ includes at least the edges induced by the synchronization that happens within the trace (but may also include additional edges)
\item $\hb$ is acyclic and consistent with $\mo$
\end{itemize}

Notice that an atomic location trace for a location $\ell$ is essentially a consistent C20 execution graph, except that the happens-before order is not fully explained by the synchronization events of the graph itself. Intuitively, it is the program's C20 execution graph, but retaining only the events pertaining to location $\ell$.

We say an event of an atomic location trace is \emph{enabled} if the event is labeled by $(t, \ell, v, o)$ such that $o$ is enabled at result $v$: $(o, v) \in \mathrm{dom}\,\mathsf{post}$.

We say an atomic location trace is \emph{fully enabled} if all events in $E$ are enabled.

We say a total tied resource $\omega$ is \emph{consistent} with an operation $o$ by a thread $t$ resulting in a value $v$ under an atomic specification $\Sigma$, denoted $\Sigma, t, v, o \vDash \omega$, if there exists a fully enabled atomic location trace under $\Sigma$ that includes an event $e$ labeled by an operation $o$ by thread $t$ resulting in $v$ and there exists a subset $E' \subseteq E$ that includes all events that happen before $e$ but does not include $e$ itself or any events that happen after $e$, such that for every total ordering of $E' \setminus \{\mathsf{init}\}$ consistent with happens before, starting from $\Sigma.\rho_0$ it is possible to, in this order, consume the tied precondition and produce the tied postcondition of each event $e' \in E'$ and arrive at $\omega$. We say that the consistency is \emph{witnessed} by the atomic location trace, $e$, and $E'$.

\subsubsection{Operational semantics}

We instrument the state space of the programming language as follows. The regular heap $h$ stores the values of nonatomic cells only; for
atomic cells the semantics does not track the value; it only tracks, in the \emph{atomic heap} $A$, each atomic location's atomic specification and total tied resource.

\begin{figure}
\begin{mathpar}
