\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{O}{ver} the past decade, mobile networks have become an essential means of accessing the Internet for billions of users worldwide. Despite a remarkable growth of mobile network coverage, significant portions of the global population remain underserved by terrestrial cellular infrastructure, particularly in remote, rural, and sparsely populated areas where the cost of deployment is prohibitive.

In recent years, particularly from 2020, Low Earth Orbit (LEO) mega-constellations have become a viable and increasingly widespread Non-Terrestrial Network (NTN) internet access solution for fixed broadband services. Networks like SpaceX's Starlink, Eutelsat's OneWeb or Amazon's Kuiper have successfully addressed longstanding limitations of traditional geostationary satellite communications. By operating at significantly lower altitudes (typically $500$--$1,200$ km), these constellations offer latency levels comparable to terrestrial networks (typically tens of milliseconds vs.~the hundreds associated with geostationary satellites), allowing real-time applications such as video conferencing and online gaming~\cite{Mohan24Local}. Furthermore, the growing number of satellites in orbit thanks to the plummeting deployment costs%\cite{Adilov2022AnSatellitesLocal} 
combined with the use of advanced beamforming technologies has contributed to significantly increasing the capacity of the system through densification and enhanced spectrum reuse.

Following these developments in fixed broadband connectivity, satellite-based cellular communications, known as Direct Satellite-to-Device (DS2D), have recently emerged as a promising solution to extend mobile network coverage. Taking advantage of the technological advancements demonstrated by LEO constellations~\cite{Su2019BroadbandTechnologies}, these systems aim to provide seamless connectivity directly to standard unmodified smartphones. Starlink, the satellite communications division of SpaceX, has become the global leader in DS2D communications. In partnership with T-Mobile, it has been the first to launch large-scale beta testing of a Supplemental Coverage from Space (SCS) service using T-Mobile's spectrum, initially limited to short messages (SMS) but planned to support voice and data services by mid-2025. % ZF: Quito esto de momento, para ahorrar sitio, se puede recuperar
% While other industry players --such as AST SpaceMobile and Lynk Global-- are also trialing DS2D technologies, Starlink’s extensive satellite constellation and mature terrestrial infrastructure provide a significant competitive advantage, consolidating its leadership in the global deployment of DS2D services.

In this article, we examine Starlink's SCS services and characterize its DS2D network —--commonly referred to by Starlink as Direct-to-Cell. %, aiming to provide timely insights into the capabilities, limitations and prospective performance and evolution of this new paradigm in mobile connectivity
Specifically, we evaluate its use by observing metrics of LTE (4G) access networks based on physical-layer measurements, namely Reference Signal Received Power (RSRP), Reference Signal Received Quality (RSRQ) and Signal-to-Interference-plus-Noise Ratio (SINR). To this end, we leverage a large crowdsourced mobile network measurement dataset provided by Weplan Analytics, comprising more than one million measurements of Starlink's DS2D network collected across the U.S. between October 2024 and April 2025. %We also use a dataset that contains measurements of T-Mobile's terrestrial network during the same period\SAA{for what?}.
Our analysis offers valuable insights into the implications of DS2D network design for mobile network planning and spectrum policy and allows projecting the performance of future data services.

The remainder of this paper is structured as follows. Section~\ref{Section:Background} provides background on the evolution of LEO satellite networks and summarizes related research. Section~\ref{Section:Methodology} describes the dataset and our analytical approach. Section~\ref{Section:Results} presents and discusses the results. Finally, Section~\ref{Section:Conclusions} offers conclusions and suggests future research.

\section{Background} \label{Section:Background}
\subsection{Evolution of LEO satellite Communication Networks}

The evolution of LEO satellite communications has progressed considerably in recent decades, driven by satellite miniaturization, improved launch capabilities, and the growing demand for ubiquitous connectivity. Early LEO constellations in the late 1990s were primarily designed to deliver voice and low-speed data services to remote and underserved areas. These systems demonstrated the feasibility of low-latency real-time communication via LEO satellites; however, their high deployment and operational costs and limited capacity hindered large-scale commercial adoption.

A new phase in LEO communications emerged in the late 2010s with the advent of mega-constellations. Initiatives such as SpaceX’s Starlink and OneWeb began deploying extensive LEO satellite networks to provide broadband internet access globally. In particular, Starlink has significantly transformed the sector by launching thousands of satellites in a short period of time. The first ones were deployed in 2019, and, by the end of 2024, the company operated more than $7,000$.
%\footnote{\url{https://planet4589.org/space/con/star/stats.html}}
Initially, Starlink adopted a bent-pipe architecture \cite{Ma2023NetworkUsersLocal}, with satellites acting as relays that amplify and forward user signals to ground stations without demodulating them. This approach enabled rapid deployment using simpler satellite designs that accelerated rollouts. However, this model imposed notable constraints, including the need for a dense network of ground stations to ensure continuous coverage and the inability to serve areas where no ground station was visible to the satellite, such as over oceans and isolated regions.

To overcome these limitations, Starlink initiated the integration of laser communications into its later first-generation satellites from 2022, and fully incorporated this technology into its second-generation (Gen2) fleet to enable Inter-Satellite Links (ISLs). This architectural advancement allowed satellites to demodulate signals onboard and dynamically route data between satellites in orbit, alleviating the need for visibility of a ground station. With this, Starlink has evolved from a ground-reliant relay network to a hybrid space-based mesh architecture, expanded its geographical reach, and improved the resilience and end-to-end performance of its network\cite{Mohan24Local}.

\subsection{Direct Satellite-to-Device (DS2D)}
% \SAA{Advanced modulation and error correction techniques combined with multibeam antennas and advanced transceivers supporting simultaneous communication with thousands of devices have enabled use cases like direct communication with regular smartphones or IoT devices in remote areas~\cite{Pasandi2024AProspectsLocal}.} \JAS{Starlink’s Direct to Cell service is enabled by key technological advancements across multiple domains, as detailed in the company’s public technical updates.
% In \textit{aerospace engineering and space sustainability}, innovations such as extremely low Earth orbit insertion—satellites initially deployed in a relatively higher LEO to mitigate early mission risks before orbit-raising—yield a $>99\%$ in-orbit satellite reliability. Improvements in satellite demisability, optical brightness mitigation, and conjunction avoidance further address sustainability and safety challenges.} Meanwhile, in \textit{radio communications and network architectures}, each D2C satellite functions as a spaceborne LTE base station, integrating inter-satellite laser backhaul with existing ground PoPs for efficient traffic routing. 

Building on general advances in LEO satellite communications, DS2D has been further enabled by significant progress in electronics, embedded systems, digital signal processing, and beamforming technologies, among others \cite{Starlink2024DS2D}. Specifically, DS2D connectivity relies on the satellites being equipped with advanced phased array antennas, highly sensitive radio receivers, and high-power transmitters. Moreover, compensation for Doppler shift and delay is essential to overcome the physical constraints of mobile-to-satellite links, and software-defined radio (SDR) is necessary to adapt the system to varying frequencies and technologies. Starlink’s DS2D architecture leverages an LTE eNodeB payload on board, enabling direct connections from smartphones using terrestrial mobile standards. Traffic can be routed either through the company's existing network of ground stations or, when necessary, across the constellation via ISLs \cite{SpaceX2023TechnicalNarrative}.

