\subsection{Related Work}
Mirroring the rapid pace of innovation of LEO satellite communication technologies, a growing body of research has emerged from multiple perspectives to explore the design, deployment, and operation of these systems. Early studies focused primarily on the foundational technologies that underpin LEO broadband services, addressing alternative network architectures (e.g., bent-pipe versus ISL designs), hybrid beamforming strategies, interference coordination mechanisms, and resource management challenges \cite{Su2019BroadbandTechnologies}. Although much of this work was initially centered on fixed satellite broadband services, recent attention has shifted toward DS2D communications \cite{Pasandi2024AProspectsLocal}, which is reflected in dedicated research and publication initiatives (see e.g. \cite{IEEECommSpecialIssueDS2D}). Alongside theoretical and architectural advances, new simulation tools, such as Hypatia and StarPerf, have been developed to support realistic modeling, design, and performance evaluation of LEO satellite networks under operational conditions.

%\footnote{\href{https://www.comsoc.org/publications/magazines/ieee-communications-magazine/cfp/direct-satellite-device-communications}{Special issue of IEEE Communications dedicated to DS2D} (last access May 2025)}.
%\footnote{See \href{https://github.com/snkas/hypatia}{Hypatia} and \href{https://github.com/SpaceNetLab/StarPerf_Simulator}{StarPerf: A Network Performance Simulator for Emerging Mega-Constellations} (last access May 2025)}

Following initial deployments and the establishment of operational networks, a growing number of measurement studies has emerged, primarily focusing on Starlink’s fixed satellite broadband services (see e.g.~\cite{Mohan24Local, MichelAPerformanceLocal, Laniewski2025MeasuringLook, Laniewski2024StarlinkEuropeLocal}). These studies have typically evaluated connectivity delivered through dedicated fixed user terminals (i.e., satellite dishes), with particular attention to metrics such as throughput and, notably, latency, a major historical limitation of traditional geostationary satellite systems.%. The emphasis on latency is particularly significant, given that it has historically been a major limitation of traditional geostationary (GEO) satellite systems.

Despite the growing body of literature on LEO satellite networks, there are, to the best of our knowledge, no empirical evaluations of commercial DS2D deployments. This paper presents the first measurement-based analysis of a real-world DS2D network, leveraging large-scale crowdsourced mobile data collected during the beta testing phase of Starlink’s service in the U.S.
\section{Methodology}
\label{Section:Methodology}
We adopt a data-driven, user-centric methodology in this study, and leverage large-scale crowdsourced mobile measurements provided by the crowdsourcing company Weplan Analytics, whose data collection methodology is specifically optimized for evaluating Radio Access Network (RAN) performance. Crowdsourced measurements offer a unique vantage point for evaluating cellular network performance, capturing real-world user experiences even in sparsely populated areas \cite{Frias2025BuildingChallengesLocal}. Subsection~\ref{Subsection:Datasources} describes the data sources and the data preprocessing; Subsection~\ref{Subsection:EstimatingSCSUse} presents how we estimate the use of SCS services; and Subsection~\ref{Subsection:methodology_performance} details how we calculate the expected performance of DS2D communications based on the SINR measurements.

\subsection{Data sources}
\label{Subsection:Datasources}
Our primary data source consists of LTE crowdsourced measurements passively collected from Android user devices between October 2024 and April 2025. All data were anonymized at collection time by the crowdsourcing provider to ensure full compliance with applicable data protection regulations. For this study, we used only the following network variables, most of them defined by the 3GPP specifications~\cite{Johnson12}.

\begin{itemize}
    \item PLMN (Public Land Mobile Network): identifier of the mobile network through the Mobile Country Code (MCC) and Mobile Network Code (MNC). We filter with $\mathrm{MCC} = 310$ and $\mathrm{MNC} = 830$ or $210$ for Starlink measurements, and $\mathrm{MNC} = 260$ for T-Mobile's terrestrial network, which will be used as a reference to benchmark Starlink’s metrics with those of terrestrial systems.

    \item EARFCN (E-UTRA Absolute Radio Frequency Channel Number).

    \item Cell bandwidth: $5$ MHz in the case of the PCS G Block.

    \item ECI (E-UTRAN Cell Identifier). 

    \item RSRP: power received from the Cell-specific Reference Signals (CRS).

    \item RSRQ: quality indicator computed as $N \cdot \mathrm{RSRP}/\mathrm{RSSI}$, where RSSI (Received Signal Strength Indicator) is the total power (including interference and noise) received within the cell's bandwidth, and $N$ is the corresponding number of resource blocks.

    \item SINR: unlike RSRP and RSRQ, SINR is only measured by the terminal when there is an active connection.

    \item Timestamp: Date and time of each measurement.

    \item GPS location: Latitude and longitude of the user terminal.

\end{itemize}



