However, a detailed analysis of these metrics reveals no significant variation or identifiable trend in the distribution of RSRP, RSRQ, or SINR measurements over the analyzed period. On the contrary, the DS2D RAN metrics remained relatively stable throughout the different deployment phases. Nonetheless, these changes are not reflected in the measured RAN indicators, likely because the network remained largely unloaded, as services were limited to SMS during this period.

A key question is how this DS2D RAN behavior differs from that based on terrestrial cellular technologies. For this, we benchmark the metrics of this network with those of T-Mobile's LTE terrestrial network. %, as described in the methodology. 
Fig.~\ref{fig:Comparison_DS2D_Terrestrial} shows the cumulative distribution functions (CDF) of RSRP, RSRQ and SINR observed in Starlink DS2D and in the terrestrial network, over the full time span of the measurements. As can be seen, users of the Starlink DS2D network experience significantly lower RSRP levels, with median values around $-121$ dBm, compared to the approximately $-97$ dBm measured in T-Mobile's terrestrial network. This disparity is primarily due to the much larger transmission distances in satellite communications, which result in DS2D RSRP values that resemble those typically observed at the cell edge in terrestrial networks. The distribution is also narrower: the interdecile range of RSRP measurements in the DS2D network is $21$ dB, vs.~$37$ dB in terrestrial networks. This reflects the more uniform link geometry: unlike terrestrial networks ---where users may be located at highly varying distances from base stations and subject to diverse propagation conditions, including outdoor and indoor--- DS2D users receive signals from satellites at more consistent distances and predominantly under line-of-sight conditions.

Regarding RSRQ, despite the limited bandwidth available to the DS2D network ($5$ MHz), values are generally more favorable for DS2D connections (median: $-9$ dB) than in terrestrial networks ($-12$ dB). This is explained by the low network load and minimal interference from other active terminals within the same coverage area, due to the current usage of the DS2D network: operation in remote areas with a limited number of users and restricted to low data-rate applications such as SMS. This situation will likely change with the introduction of voice and data services in the second half of 2025, or in the event of increased demand during large-scale outages of terrestrial infrastructure.

We observe that the correlation between RSRP and RSRQ is stronger in Starlink's DS2D network than in the terrestrial network, with computed Pearson correlation coefficients of $0.66$ and $0.34$ respectively. This is consistent with the fact that network load is lower in the former than in the latter. With lower load, variations in the interference levels are smaller, which makes RSRP and RSRQ more correlated. 

Lastly, the SINR measurements reveal a median value of $0$~dB and an interdecile range of $16$ dB. These are again lower than the typical values observed in the terrestrial network, of $5$ dB and $20$ dB, respectively, as a result of the low-load, coverage-limited nature of the DS2D network.

Building on these observations, we next evaluate the potential performance of future DS2D data services.% beyond the current SMS-only phase.

\subsection{Performance assessment of future DS2D services}
\label{subsect:performance_assessment}

To assess the performance of future DS2D services, we make use of the modified Shannon capacity formula with the measured SINR and available bandwidth, as described in Subsection \ref{Subsection:methodology_performance}. The measured SINR values reported above are almost insensitive to the three options described for computing $m$, because large values of the SINR are infrequent in the DS2D network, and thus $m$ has a negligible impact on the results. Thus, the measured SINR mean and median values translate into spectral efficiency values of $0.79$ and $0.64$ bps/Hz, respectively. As the DS2D service currently operates in the PCS G block only, which offers $2\times 5$ MHz of bandwidth, this estimated spectral efficiency corresponds to an estimated downlink capacity of $4$ Mbps per beam, meaning the capacity a single user who occupies the entire beam bandwidth would experience outdoor in a remote or sparsely populated area.

%\footnote{https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/t-mobile-and-spacex-plan-to-connect-mobile-phones-from-space/}. 

While the estimated DS2D speed may be sufficient to support basic connectivity in remote or underserved areas, it remains significantly below the average download speed experienced in terrestrial networks. This gap underscores the complementary nature of DS2D services in its current phase, aligning with its intended role as a SCS solution. Nevertheless, performance is expected to improve in the coming months through a combination of increased radiated power, continued expansion of the satellite constellation, and greater bandwidth allocation per cell.

As noted above, the FCC authorized a $10$-dB increase in OOBE for DS2D transmissions in March 2025. Although measurements from April 2025 do not yet show any improvement in SINR or other metrics, this regulatory change could enable SINR gains in the near future. The expected gain is less than $10$ dB, because, on one hand, transmitter non-linearities require an increased power back-off with the new OOBE limit; and, on the other hand, the increased radiated can lead to significantly higher interference. The latter effect is expected to be small, as currently performance is mainly constrained by received power and noise rather than by interference. As for the former, SpaceX estimates\footnote{See \url{https://www.fcc.gov/ecfs/document/10914226871971/1}} that the increased OOBE limit would improve SINR by $3.1$ dB. Assuming this gain, the estimated spectral efficiency rises to $1.17$ (mean) and $1.05$ bps/Hz (median), i.e.~it improves by a $1.5$ factor, which results in around $5.9$ Mbps. As expected, this aligns with the well-established principle that increasing SINR yields significant capacity gains in coverage-limited scenarios.

In Subsection~\ref{Subsection:methodology_performance}, a maximum MCS has been assumed corresponding to $256$-QAM. The 3GPP standard contemplates the use of even higher order modulation. However, this will not produce a noticeable capacity improvement. The reason is, again, the coverage-limited nature of the network: large values of SINR occur very infrequently with the existing SINR distribution, and thus high MCS are hardly ever used. This is the case even if the previously discussed increase in the OOBE limit is incorporated.

Throughput in the DS2D network may also be increased by expanding the available bandwidth. Assuming that the additional spectrum is paired with proportional transmit power, the spectral efficiency remains constant, and the per-beam capacity doubles. Starlink could pursue this by accessing more mobile spectrum (IMT) for the SCS services, but %, in line with T-Mobile's PCS G Block used now 
due to the risk of interference, this would require terrestrial operators to release spectrum, which is unlikely.
%\SAA{No podría también llegar a más acuerdos? Lo que también es unlikely porque que solo tengan 5MHz ya les va bien a los que mandan en espectro} 
Alternatively, Starlink may seek to use Mobile Satellite Service (MSS) spectrum and has, in fact, requested access to several bands around 2 GHz (specifically, the 1.6/2.4 GHz bands and the 2020–2025 MHz Earth-to-space band). However, this request was denied by the FCC in March 2024.\footnote{See FCC DA-24-300} %\footnote{\url{https://www.fcc.gov/document/spacex-gen2-mss-application-dismissed-unacceptable-filing}}. 
If an additional $2\times5$ MHz were granted in the future, the estimated mean throughput could increase from $4$ Mbps to $8$ Mbps and could potentially reach values close to $12$ Mbps when combined with the increase in the OOBE limit.

%\SAA{Uso de tecnologías de 5G o 6G? Ej. aquí hablan de satellite swarms -- https://athene-forschung.unibw.de/doc/144338/144338.pdf -- para, y cito `provide an enhanced gain, narrower beam width and lower launch/build costs compared to conventional single satellite systems with large phased antenna arrays'. A lo mejor se puede ligar con lo que dice Zoraida de lanzar más satélites}

%\ZFB{Revisar si hay previstos más lanzamientos de satétlites Gen2 con DS2D para valorar si eso podría aumentar la capacidad del sistema. La FCC ha autorizado hasta $7500$ satélites}

% \SAA{Este párrafo se puede resumir mucho. yo empezaría diciendo que Starlink is requesting authorization to raise emissions limit by 10dB - y añadir a continuación una referencia/link a la FCC} Spectral efficiency is expected to improve if out-of-band emissions limit can be increased by $10$ dB, as mentioned in Subsection~\ref{part: 10-dB increase}. This improvement can be approximately assessed by assuming a $10$-dB increase in SINR. This is slightly optimistic for two reasons. Firstly, the authorization of $10$-dB larger out-of-band emissions does not necessarily lead to an increase of transmit power by the same factor, because out-of-band emissions could increase by a larger factor due to transmitter non-linearities. Secondly, a larger transmit power can also increase interference, resulting in the SINR increasing less than the transmit power. However, these effects are expected to be small, considering that the network is coverage-limited, where transmit power is restricted by the out-of-band emission limit rather than by transmitter limitations, and noise is more dominates over interference. Thus the spectral efficiency computed with $10$-dB larger SINR values should be considered as an upper bound, but not far from the actual values. The resulting average and median spectral efficiency values are $2.35$ and $2.38$ b/s/Hz respectively, which represents a three-fold increase. This is in accordance with the well known fact that in a coverage-limited scenario an increase in SINR leads to a significant increase in capacity.

In the longer term, satellite swarm array antennas show promising preliminary results in increasing the directivity of satellite beams~\cite{Tuzi23SatelliteSwarm}, which allows for higher gains and improved spatial multiplexing. Achieving this would require the launch and operation of many more satellites, which would also increase the overall throughput of DS2D networks and the effective capacity experienced by end users. Although the FCC denied Starlink’s request to authorize 30,000 DS2D-capable satellites, the company is still authorized to operate up to 7,500 such satellites, indicating substantial remaining potential for network densification.
%However, expanding satellite constellations requires the authorization of regulatory authorities, which is currently preventing Starlink from deploying additional Gen2 Starlink satellites and reaching a fleet of $30000$.\footnote{See FCC's DA 24-1193}

\section{Conclusions} 
\label{Section:Conclusions}
This paper presented the first empirical analysis of a commercially deployed DS2D network, focusing on Starlink’s Supplemental Coverage from Space Service in the U.S. in partnership with T-Mobile. Using large-scale crowdsourced measurements between October 2024 and April 2025, we characterized the spatio-temporal evolution and RAN performance of the DS2D network, which operated under an SMS-only configuration during the observed period.

Our results show a clear correlation between the number of DS2D-capable satellites launched and the number of unique cell identifiers observed, and spatial patterns that align well with the operator's coverage gaps%, reinforcing the role of Starlink's Direct-to-Cell as an SCS solution
. We observed stable distributions of RSRP, RSRQ, and SINR across deployment phases, with signal power levels reflecting the expected limitations of satellite-based communication, and enhanced signal quality due to low traffic demand during this initial SMS-only phase. Based on SINR measurements, we estimate a current mean throughput of approximately $4$ Mbps, which is functional for basic services but significantly below terrestrial averages. Overall, the findings underscore the role of Starlink's DS2D network as a solution for providing SCS.%still complementary, not substitutive, nature of current DS2D connectivity. 

Looking ahead, throughput and performance may improve to $12$ Mbps with higher radiated power (as recently authorized by the FCC), wider bandwidth allocation, and continued constellation expansion. However, these enhancements will depend on regulatory developments ---particularly spectrum access--- and on the evolution of service demand. 

% DS2D services have recently entered the scene of the telecommunications industry offering a practical cost-effective way to provide mobile connectivity from space to regular smartphones in remote areas or as a parallel infrastructure for critical communications (e.g., in the event of a disaster). In this paper we have shown that crowdsourced measurements can provide a unique perspective on the evolution of their deployment, and provide valuable insights into the quality of DS2D networks, and existing and potential DS2D services.

% Despite the current technical and regulatory limitations, we point to technical, regulatory and policy measures that can contribute to developing this cutting-edge technology. These include regulatory decisions like making available more spectrum, allowing for additional transmitting power while ensuring co-existence with terrestrial mobile services; and technical breakthroughs like X, Y, Z...

Finally, our work illustrates the value of crowdsourced data to monitor emerging network technologies. %Despite the common expectations of an urban bias in crowdsourced data, our findings confirm its utility for aggregate analysis in rural and remote areas, precisely where SCS services are most impactful. 
Beyond characterizing deployment and performance, such data can help detect underserved zones, assess coverage gaps, and inform infrastructure planning, offering a promising tool for both technical and policy research on next-generation mobile systems. Future work will involve monitoring the evolution of DS2D networks and services, tracking the evolution of Starlink’s network as new services are introduced, and exploring other emerging DS2D deployments.


