Several factors contribute to the failure of these models, and they are key reasons why these approaches do not work effectively in our data. Additionally, we propose solutions to address these issues in our model:

\begin{enumerate}

    % Didn't filter spammers spammer,inverse experts
    \item Filtering spammers: Neither of these models applied effective filtering to remove noise generated by automated bots or spammers. We discovered that social media platforms contain posts from bots or spammers, characterized by repetitive posts at fixed intervals (e.g., publishing content around every 24 seconds), all carrying sentiment labels. These spammers can post hundreds or even thousands of tweets with sentiment labels per day about the same stock. These excessive posts distort sentiment analysis and need to be partially filtered. To address this issue, for each user-stock pair, we only retain the post closest to market closing time on any given day.

    \item Sufficient Past Data Criteria: Existing methods employ insufficient criteria for expert identification, either using a fixed number of $N$ past posts (e.g., $N$=10) \cite{liao2014winning} or considering prediction accuracy only over a short period of $K$ days (e.g., $K$=40 or 90) \cite{A_user_centric,aaai_expert_based}. However, we found these methods unreliable. In our dataset, even after filtering out spammers, users who achieved over 80\% accuracy in their past 20 posts had an average accuracy of only 54.0\%, and users who achieved 80\% accuracy in the past 40 days had an average accuracy of only 53.9\% during the testing phase. Further analysis revealed that most users only have high prediction accuracy during specific periods, after which their accuracy declines significantly, and vice versa. For example, users who posted for more than 20 days in 2022 with an accuracy above 80\% had an average accuracy about 50\% in 2023, while users who had accuracy below 20\% in 2022 saw their accuracy rise to nearly 50\% in 2023. To better understand the shortcomings of existing approaches and address these limitations in expert identification, we propose two key criteria:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Precise prediction across different market regimes}: Some users consistently post only bullish predictions every day, while others post only bearish predictions. For those posting only bullish predictions, when the stock is in an upward trend, they may appear to be experts due to their constant bullish posts, but during market corrections, these same bullish posts lead to poor predictions, effectively turning them into inverse experts. Similarly, users who consistently post bearish predictions may seem expert-like during market downturns but become inverse experts during upward trends. These users have little value for analysis as they do not base their predictions on real market insight. To address this issue, not only do we consider the accuracy of the past $N$ tweets to ensure the dynamism of the experts, but we also need to look at their long-term predictions (two years) and see if they maintain a high level of accuracy, as most stocks will not remain in the same trend over an extended period of time.

        \item \textbf{Focused Expert Evaluation}: True market experts typically specialize in a manageable number of stocks to maintain deep analysis and insight. However, some users post multiple bullish or bearish sentiments for a large number of stocks on the same day. If the market happens to move in their predicted direction, they will appear highly accurate and be incorrectly identified as experts in previously mentioned expert identification methods. Such broad, unfocused posting behavior contradicts the nature of genuine expertise, which requires concentrated attention and thorough analysis. To ensure we identify truly focused experts, we require that a user's past 20 posts must span at least five different trading days \footnote{we tested various parameters and found that five different days yielded the best performance considering both the coverage and accuracy}. This helps filter out users who spread their predictions too thin across many stocks without demonstrating sustained, focused analysis. 
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

Furthermore, previous models focused solely on identifying experts who consistently made correct predictions, ignoring the possibility of identifying inverse experts. We have added inverse experts to our approach, where we identify these inverse experts and take the opposite action based on their opinions.

By utilizing this approach, we developed a dynamic expert tracing system. For each trading day, if there is an expert whose past predictions meet the aforementioned criteria, we treat that expert's prediction for the day as a representative trend signal, serving as the pseudo ground truth for whether the stock will rise or fall. 

The procedure is summarized in Algorithm ~\ref{algorithm}. For each trading day $d$, we first filter the posts to retain only the latest post per user-stock pair. For users who posted on day $d$, we evaluate their prediction accuracy over two time horizons. Let $T_{i,recent}$ and $F_{i,recent}$ represent the number of correct and incorrect predictions in their most recent $N$ posts, while $T_{i,long}$ and $F_{i,long}$ denote these numbers over the past $T$ years.
To ensure both recent performance and consistent long-term accuracy, we require experts to meet two criteria: their most recent $N$ ($N$=20) posts must span at least $K$ ($K$=5) different days and achieve an accuracy above $P_2$ (80\%), while maintaining a long-term accuracy above $P_1$ (65\%) over the past $T$ ($T$=2) years. Similarly, inverse experts are identified when their recent and long-term accuracies fall below 1-$P_2$ (20\%) and 1-$P_1$ (35\%) respectively.

% The expert tracing system achieved an accuracy rate as high as 77.26\%, demonstrating its effectiveness in enhancing stock prediction models by filtering out noise and focusing on both expert and inverse expert insights.
The expert tracing system achieved an accuracy rate as high as 77.26\%. Furthermore, the inference stage has a time complexity of $O(len(C_d))$ where $\text{len}(C_d)$ is the number of posts on the given day d. Since our Stocktwits database contains the historical posting data and accuracy for each user, the inference phase simply involves traversing all posts once to update the relevant information for each user. Therefore, the time taken to complete this operation scales linearly with the total number of posts. The low complexity, combined with the high accuracy, demonstrates the effectiveness and efficiency of our method in enhancing stock prediction models by filtering out noise and focusing on both expert and inverse expert insights.


\begin{algorithm}[t!]
\caption{Expert Tracing System for Date $d$}
\label{algorithm}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE current date $d$, minimum post requirement $N$, minimum days span $K$, tweet set $C$ with $C_d \subset C$ denoting tweets posted on day $d$, long-term accuracy threshold $P_1$, short-term accuracy threshold $P_2$, long-term range $T$
\ENSURE expert and inverse expert extraction for date $d$

\STATE $C_d \leftarrow \text{filter\_daily\_latest}(C_d)$ \COMMENT{Keep only the latest post per user-stock pair on day d}
\STATE $I_d \leftarrow \text{all users of } C_d$
% \STATE $I_d \leftarrow \text{all\_users}(C_d)$

\FOR{each $i \in I_d$}
    \STATE $C_{i,recent} \leftarrow \text{get\_recent\_N\_posts}(C, i, N)$ \COMMENT{Get most recent N posts for user $i$}
    \STATE $days_{i,recent} \leftarrow \text{count\_unique\_days}(C_{i,recent})$
    \IF{$days_{i,recent} < K$}
        \STATE continue
    \ENDIF

    \STATE $T_{i,recent} \leftarrow 0$; $F_{i,recent} \leftarrow 0$ \COMMENT{Recent N posts accuracy counters}
    \FOR{each $c \in C_{i,recent}$}
        \STATE Check stock price change $\rho$ on the next trading day
        \IF{$(c \in \text{bullish} \land \rho \in \text{rise}) \lor (c \in \text{bearish} \land \rho \in \text{fall})$}
            \STATE $T_{i,recent} \leftarrow T_{i,recent} + 1$
        \ELSE
            \STATE $F_{i,recent} \leftarrow F_{i,recent} + 1$
        \ENDIF
    \ENDFOR
    \STATE $A_{i,recent} \leftarrow T_{i,recent}/(T_{i,recent} + F_{i,recent})$

    \STATE $C_{i,long} \leftarrow \text{get\_posts}(C_{d-T:d}, i)$ \COMMENT{Get all posts in past T years}
    \STATE $T_{i,long} \leftarrow 0$; $F_{i,long} \leftarrow 0$ \COMMENT{Long-term accuracy counters}
    \FOR{each $c \in C_{i,long}$}
        \STATE Check stock price change $\rho$ on the next trading day
        \IF{$(c \in \text{bullish} \land \rho \in \text{rise}) \lor (c \in \text{bearish} \land \rho \in \text{fall})$}
            \STATE $T_{i,long} \leftarrow T_{i,long} + 1$
        \ELSE
            \STATE $F_{i,long} \leftarrow F_{i,long} + 1$
        \ENDIF
    \ENDFOR
    \STATE $A_{i,long} \leftarrow T_{i,long}/(T_{i,long} + F_{i,long})$

    \IF{$A_{i,recent} \geq P_2$ \AND $A_{i,long} \geq P_1$}
        \STATE Mark $i$ as expert and take long (short) positions for bullish (bearish) sentiment
    \ELSIF{$A_{i,recent} \leq (1-P_2)$ \AND $A_{i,long} \leq (1-P_1)$}
        \STATE Mark $i$ as inverse expert and take short (long) positions for bullish (bearish) sentiment
    \ENDIF
\ENDFOR

\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}


\section{Expert Opinion Propagation}
Despite integrating both expert and inverse expert signals, like other expert identification approaches \cite{aaai_expert_based,Forecating_stock_prices,A_user_centric}, our model still suffers from signal sparsity, producing meaningful predictions for only a few stocks on certain trading days. Although these predictions serve as a pseudo ground truth for stock trends, they account for only about 4\% of our dataset, leaving most data points unlabeled. To address this limitation, we propose a graph-based method that transforms these limited expert predictions into practical signals and propagates them across related stocks, thereby extending coverage to inform all data points in the dataset. 


\subsection{Trend Signals Transformation}
Though predicting whether a stock will rise or fall is important, the ultimate goal is not simply trend forecasting but maximizing excess returns through investment strategies. Feng et al. \cite{feng2019temporal} has pointed out that traditional stock prediction methods focus either on classification (predicting the direction) or regression (predicting the price) are suboptimal because they fail to align with the goal of selecting stocks with the highest potential returns, which is the real objective for investors. Given that a more practical approach to stock prediction involves optimizing return ratios rather than focusing solely on trend direction, it becomes necessary to transform the pseudo ground truth from binary trend indicators (up or down) into continuous return ratios which can be treated as expert signal.

The transformation is as follows: 
for stocks predicted by experts to rise, we compute the average return ratio from days within the past 30 days when the stock showed an upward trend. Similarly, for a predicted downward trend, we use the average of the down days' return ratios within the same period. This 30-day window for the return ratio which acts as a monthly indicator is a common approach in financial modelling for stock prediction \cite{Forecasting_significant_stock,Stock_market_prediction,A_systematic_review} as it helps to smooth out daily volatility while providing a sufficient sample size to capture recent market trends. This calculated average thus serves as a `prior' for return ratios, which effectively functions as the expert signal. If a stock is not predicted by experts on any given day, then the `prior' for that stock is set to 0.


 \subsection{Temporal Pre-training Model}
Now that we have obtained the sparse prior for the return ratios, the challenge remains that for over 95\% of the stock-day pairs, we still lack any prior information for these pairs as they have no expert signal. To address this, we propose an initial step to train a temporal model, which will serve as a baseline for generating return ratio estimates. This step acts similarly to a pre-training process, where the temporal model captures patterns in stock movements without direct reliance on expert signals. 

 The model is trained using historical stock price data (open, high, low, close prices, and trading volume) and fundamental data. Unlike many studies that follow Feng et al.'s \cite{feng2019temporal, Deep_attentive_learning,wang2022hatr} method of normalizing the entire dataset across the whole time dimension (including test sets), which may lead to test set leakage, we normalize each feature daily across the stock cross-section.

