\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\subsubsection{Dataset}
In our study, the expert signals come from StockTwits, a social media platform designed specifically for investors and traders to share insights. Users can post messages related to individual stocks, each of which has its own discussion thread for comments. These messages are often tagged with sentiments like ``bullish" or ``bearish," reflecting the user’s outlook on the stock. StockTwits merges social media engagement with stock market discussions, making it a valuable tool for capturing real-time investor sentiment.

We accessed this data through the StockTwits API\footnote{https://api.stocktwits.com/api/2/streams/symbol/{stock}.json}, specifically using the endpoint to fetch publicly available data related to specific stocks. During collection, we implemented an automatic pagination mechanism in our data retrieval script. This allowed us to capture both the most recent and complete historical data from the forum.

To optimize the efficiency of our data retrieval process, we employed a distributed system approach using proxy services, enabling multi-threaded, multi-node data collection. This setup significantly accelerated our data gathering efforts; a single node can collect one day's data from the forum in about 30 minutes. By employing 30 nodes simultaneously, we significantly accelerated the data collection process, retrieving all daily posts within a minute. This substantial increase in speed ensured that we could quickly gather the latest data, enabling timely mining of expert signals to facilitate subsequent model training.

The collected data contains a vast amount of valuable information, with each tweet corresponding to a user’s opinion about a specific stock. For each tweet, the data includes the content, the stock it refers to (including its associated industry, sector, symbol, and the exchange where it is listed), the creation time of the tweet, the user ID, the number of likes it received, the sentiment expressed by the user (either bullish or bearish), and the retweet information. Our data collection spanned from 2010 to the end of 2023, and we have established a database to store all the collected data. This allows users to access various StockTwits data directly from the database without the need to write complex scripts for data extraction. Stock price data was sourced from Polygon.io\footnote{https://polygon.io}, and fundamental data from EODHD\footnote{https://eodhd.com}.

For the pre-training of our temporal model, we utilized the collected stock prices and fundamental data spanning from 2010 to 2018. To help enrich our expert signal collection and avoid isolated nodes in the industry graph, we augmented our dataset with 10 highly discussed stocks beyond the index constituents. Our DualGAT model was trained and validated using data from 2019 to 2021 and  conducted extensive testing of our DualGAT model on three distinct datasets: the Nasdaq 100 index, S\&P 500 index, and the updated StockNet dataset by \cite{www2024learning}, using data from 2022 to 2023 as the test sets.

\subsubsection{Baseline}
In this paper, we employ three different baselines to evaluate the effectiveness of our model:

\noindent \textbf{1. Expert signal baseline:} As illustrated in Figure 1, the expert selection methods proposed by Wang et al.\cite{aaai_expert_based} in the MFN model, the smart user classification model proposed by Coyne et al. \cite{Forecating_stock_prices}, the user centric method proposed by Bouadjenek et al. \cite{A_user_centric} and the Wining model proposed by Liao et al. \cite{liao2014winning} were selected as manually designed baseline models to assess the accuracy of the expert signals.

\noindent \textbf{2. Simple message propagation baseline:} To demonstrate the effectiveness of the message-passing capability in our designed DualGAT, we employ the simplest form of message passing without graph neural network architectures, denoted as $Wx$, where $W$ is the normalized adjacency matrix derived from either our industry or correlation matrix, and $x$ represents the stock's return ratio prior mentioned before. Similar to DualGAT, this message-passing method also performs a two-hop propagation. This process helps to diffuse the return ratio prior from nodes (stocks) with expert signals (expert-derived pseudo labels) to adjacent nodes, thereby leveraging the structural connections of the graph to enhance the predictive ability. This baseline is crucial for understanding the ability of the expert signal independently as a trading signal. At the same time, it also serves as the baseline for our DualGAT model that combines traditional financial features.


\noindent \textbf{3. Temporal or spatial-temporal model comparison baseline:} To demonstrate the effectiveness of our expert-driven approach combined with DualGAT, this baseline employs other models known for temporal and spatial-temporal predictions in time series forecasting or stock prediction. We use these models to train and compare performance on the same training, validation and test sets.


