\section{Conclusions}

In this paper, we present VoxAging, a large-scale longitudinal 
% audiovisual
dataset. It includes recordings from 293 speakers (226 English and 67 Mandarin) over a span of 17 years, totaling 7,522 hours, with weekly samples. Our analysis of speaker aging reveals that the performance of speaker verification systems deteriorates with age. Improving the performance of speaker recognition models can enhance their resistance to speaker aging. Additionally, speaker similarity scores significantly declines over time. The impact of age and gender on speaker aging shows that 40$\sim$50 age group and female group exhibit more pronounced voice deterioration.

