\subsection{Pressure Data and Audio Corpus} 
\label{subsec:Speech_Corpus}
\vspace{-0.5em}


We use VCTK (version 0.92) \cite{yamagishi2019cstr}, a multi-speaker English corpus for evaluation. The dataset contains $\sim$43600 audio traces sampled at 48 kHz from 110 individuals (i.e.,
balanced for males and females). We downsample the dataset to 8 kHz for evaluation. Each audio clip has a duration ranging from 2s to 7s. We standardize all audio clips to 4s by either zero-padding or silence trimming. Following \cite{kumar2020nu}, only the mic1 data are used for the experiments, and p280 and p315 are omitted due to technical issues. We play the audio at 60 dB through a loudspeaker and collect the corresponding pressure data via a DPS sensor (SDP810-125PA). The speaker is placed at a 5 cm distance from one of the pressure ports to play the audio clips.  To prevent sound signals from influencing the pressure in the other port, we connect a sampling tube to it, isolated by positioning its opening a meter away. Signals from the sensor are collected as .edf and next converted to .wav format using a custom Python script. The sampling frequency of the pressure sensor is varied in between 500 Hz and 2 kHz. We use sinc interpolation to upsample before the audio reconstruction to ensure that the system input and output have the same shape.  Note that in a real case, the speech contents may be different from the spoken ones during the attack phase. Thus, for testing purposes, we use 11 different speakers, who are not present in the training. The models are trained offline with an NVIDIA 4090 GPU and deployed on a desktop for speech recovery. 

%which features a 16,000 Hz sampling rate and recordings made in a semi-anechoic chamber. Each speaker contributed about 400 sentences, with durations ranging from 2 to 7 seconds. To streamline the training process, we standardized all audio clips to 4 seconds by either zero-padding or trimming them. Additionally, we applied a sixth-order Butterworth low-pass filter to eliminate frequencies above 1 KHz while retaining the 16 KHz sampling rate. The resulting bandlimited audio was then used as input for the model to predict the missing high-frequency components.

