\subsection{Data-driven supervised learning approach}
Here we present the results of using the supervised data-driven approach, i.e, the loss function (MAE) is evaluated at the 64 grid points using the given true electric potential points, as described in Eq.~\eqref{eq:data_loss}. The training data is generated from a baseline PIC code with initial stream velocities $v_0 = [0.03, 0.05, 0.1]$. The tests are made on the PIC simulation with a new unseen stream velocity $v_0 = 0.07$, which should reveal the generalizing capacity of the neural networks under consideration. 
%In Fig. \ref{fig:trainig}, we show the evolution of loss values (MAE) for the training data for the three models considered and run for 500 epochs. We have independently made certain that there is no over-fitting by tracking the validation loss during the training. We identify that the CQC model converges to a lower error when compared to the CCC model.

In Figure~\ref{fig:predicts}, we present the electric field prediction results for different PIC iterations. The left figure shows the electric fields $E(x)$ predictions for some of the time steps when compared with the ones obtained using traditional methods ($\Phi_{exact}$). We observe that the hybrid CQC model can fit the data well, while the classical data deviates from the correct solutions in some of the instances. The plots also demonstrate the highly non-linear nature of the electric fields in question and the diversity in their shapes, which the hybrid neural network is able to capture efficiently. We compare the overall predictions for the 1000 time steps of the simulations in the boxplot shown on the right in Figure~\ref{fig:predicts}. The plot shows the distribution of the mean relative absolute errors, MRAE (the relative metric is chosen because the electric field amplitudes lie within a large range of values), where the error values by a CQC model has a lower median value ($\approx 0.168$) and the smaller outliers than the classical CCC model (median $\approx 0.265$).

Further, in Figure~\ref{fig:phase-space}, we show the phase space of the particles at the end of the simulation for $v_0 = 0.07$. The instability results in a vortex-like structure in the phase space of the particles, which is characteristic of the 2-stream instability problem. One can see that both CQC and CCC models result in a phase space similar to the baseline PIC simulation, however, a careful observation of the velocity distribution $f(v)$ as shown in the histogram reveals that CQC leads to a phase space that is closer to the traditional PIC simulation.

We gain further insights into the problem by comparing the total energy of the plasma throughout simulations, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:energy-eamplitude}. The total energy of the particles during the simulation should be conserved, and the numerical value should be $\approx 0.0024$. While in the initial phase, both models conserve energy; at later times, the total energy is not conserved. We see that the CQC-PIC code shows an energy fluctuation that is slightly smaller when compared to the energy fluctuations of the CCC-PIC code. In addition, we also track the electric field amplitudes during the evolution. The electric field amplitudes are similar to the traditional PIC simulation, with the exponential growth of the electric field in the initial phase and the saturation of amplitudes when the instability is fully developed \cite{Hou:2streaminstability}. 




