\subsection{Physics-informed learning approach}
In this section, we present the results obtained using physics-informed neural networks integrated with PIC code for the 2-stream instability problem. In this case, the neural networks are trained with the loss function $\mathcal{L}_\mathrm{PINN}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:pinn_loss}. We observe improved accuracy in predicting electric fields, particularly in the case of classical neural networks, which previously yielded relatively inaccurate results under the purely data-driven approach, as discussed in the previous section.

We use $N_d = 64$ data points and the Poisson PDE loss evaluated at $N_g = 64$ points in Eq. \eqref{eq:pinn_loss}. The gradients are computed using \verb|torch.gradient()| module. We use a relative weight $\lambda=0.3$ (for CCC) and $\lambda=0.5$ (for CQC) between data loss and physics loss, and the models are trained for 3000 epochs. In Fig. \ref{fig:pinn} we present the results of errors in predicting true electric fields for the case of $v_0 = 0.07$, where the errors are computed against the true solutions obtained from the traditional methods. We compare the performance of both the CQC and CCC neural networks when trained with $\mathcal{L}_\mathrm{PINN}$ and $\mathcal{L}_\mathrm{data}$. The physics-informed approach shows an advantage for the classical CCC model in predicting the electric field; however, when the trained model is integrated into the PIC iterations, there is no particular advantage in phase space. For the hybrid model, the improvement is marginal in electric field predictions, and the resultant PIC evolution showed slightly lower fluctuations in total energy when compared to the data-driven approach. Additionally, the PINN method should yield reliable results even with a reduced number of data points $N_d$, which is particularly advantageous in practical scenarios where only sparse data is available for training the neural network.

Furthermore, we have used the finite difference method (which is implemented using \verb|torch.gradient()|) to compute the gradients within the physics loss as we are dealing with the discrete set of $\Phi$ values of the neural network output. Using more efficient methods, such as automatic differentiation techniques to define $\mathcal{L}_\mathrm{PINN}$, can improve the physics-informed strategy and make it more effective.

We test the physics-informed HNN approach for another special case of 2-streams of electrons, where the two beams are initialized with a normal distribution of thermal velocities $v_{th}$ with mean equal to 0. In this case, theoretically, there should not be any growth in the induced electric field, and the plasma should remain stable. The total energy is also conserved in such a system. The CCC and CQC models are trained with the data of $v_{th} = [0.01, 0.03, 0.07, 0.1]$. We make the test hybrid PIC simulation for the case of $v_{th} = 0.05 $. In Figure~\ref{fig:v0_0}, we show the results of total energy and the final velocity distribution. Both CQC and CCC lead to small energy fluctuations, with CQC showing lower fluctuation than CCC. Both the classical and hybrid models result in a similar velocity distribution at the end of the simulation and match well with that of the traditional PIC simulation.


