\section{An Application of ACP of Codes to the Two-User Binary Adder Channel}\label{sec:app}

In this section, we demonstrate how an ACP of codes can be effectively utilized for coding in the noiseless two-user binary adder channel. Let $\{\CC, \CD\}$ be a pair of additive $S$-codes such that their direct sum satisfies $\CC \oplus_{R} \CD = S^n$. This condition implies that every vector $ a \in \FF_{q^m}^n$ can be uniquely expressed as the sum of vectors from $\CC$ and $\CD$, i.e., there exist unique $c \in \CC$ and $ d \in \CD$ such that $a = c + d$.

Given this decomposition, we can define an orthogonal projector $\Upsilon: S^n \to \CC$ with respect to $\CD$, which maps a vector $a$ to its unique component $ c \in \CC$ in the sum $ a = c + d$. We now formalize this result through the following proposition.

\begin{prop}
Let $\CC$ and $\CD$ be additive $S$-codes with generator matrices $\mathrm{G}_1$ and $\mathrm{G}_2$, and parity-check matrices $\mathrm{H}_1$ and $\mathrm{H}_2$, respectively. Suppose $\rk_{R}(\CC) + \rk_{R}(\CD) = 2n$ and the matrix
\[
\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{G}_1^{\top}\right)
\]
is invertible. Then $\{\CC, \CD\}$ forms an ACP of codes. Moreover, the orthogonal projector $\Upsilon: \FF_{q^m}^n \to \CC$ is given by
\[
\Upsilon( a) = \mathrm{Tr}\left( a \mathrm{H}_2^{\top}\right) \mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathrm{G}_1\mathrm{H}_2^{\top}\right)^{-1} \mathrm{G}_1.
\]
\end{prop}

\begin{proof} 
The pair $\{\CC, \CD\}$ forms an ACP. Hence, for any $ a \in \FF_{q^m}^n$, there exist unique vectors $c \in \CC$ and $d \in \CD$ such that $a = c + d$ and $\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{G}_1^{\top}\right)$ is invertible. Also, we notice that $\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathrm{G}_1\mathrm{H}_2^{\top}\right)$ is invertible. 
So, now applying the definition of $\Upsilon$, we get
\[
\Upsilon( a) = \mathrm{Tr}\left( a \mathrm{H}_2^{\top}\right) \mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathrm{G}_1\mathrm{H}_2^{\top}\right)^{-1} \mathrm{G}_1.
\]
Since $c \in \CC$ and $d \in \CD$, there exist vectors $x$ and $y$ such that $c = x \mathrm{G}1$ and $d = y \mathrm{H}_2$. Substituting $a = c + d$, we have
\begin{align*}
\Upsilon(\ba) &= \mathrm{Tr}\left( (x\mathrm{G}_1+y\mathrm{G}_2) \mathrm{H}_2^{\top}\right) \mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathrm{G}_1\mathrm{H}_2^{\top}\right)^{-1} \mathrm{G}_1 \\
&= x\mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathrm{G}_1 \mathrm{H}_2^{\top}\right) \mathrm{Tr}\left(\mathrm{G}_1\mathrm{H}_2^{\top}\right)^{-1} \mathrm{G}_1\\
&= x \mathrm{G}_1= c.
\end{align*}
Thus, $\Upsilon$ is the desired orthogonal projector from $\FF_{q^m}^n$ onto $\CC$ with respect to $\CD$.
\end{proof}

Now, consider a communication system based on the noiseless two-user binary adder channel over $S$. Let $\{\CC, \CD\}$ be a pair of additive codes forms an ACP. Suppose user 1 sends a codeword $c \in \CC$, and user 2 sends a codeword $d \in \CD$. The received vector is their component-wise sum:
\[
a = c + d.
\]
To recover the original codewords from $a$, the receiver applies the orthogonal projector $\Upsilon$ to obtain
\[
\Upsilon(a) = c.
\]
The second codeword is then computed by subtraction
\[
d = a - c.
\]
This decoding procedure successfully recovers both transmitted codewords $c$ and $d$ from the received vector $a$ in the noiseless setting.



