The feature selection and explainable clustering problems are {\em static problems}; i.e., the algorithms for these problems receive all of their inputs before returning the output. This is in contrast to the nearest neighbor problem which is a {\em data structure problem}. 
%We believe that we are the first to study geometric data structures in this ultra-dimensional regime.) 
Nevertheless, given the connections between clustering and nearest neighbor problems, techniques from some of the previous works on these static problems can be extended to the nearest neighbor problem. (All data structures discussed here are randomized and answer each query correctly with $\Omega(1)$ probability. The space complexity refers to the number of words and the time is in the word-RAM model. $\tilde O(f(n))$ hides $\polylog(f(n))$ factors.)


