We start with the basic observation that computing a relative approximation of the distance between a single input point and a query point (unknown at the  time of preprocessing) requires accessing all coordinates because the two points may differ in only a single, unknown coordinate. Interestingly enough, we can still \emph{compare} distances to multiple input points in sublinear time and space.\danupon{Are we sure that the readers will find this interesting? If not, it might backfire.} To illustrate this, consider a query point~$q$ and two input points $c_1$ and $c_2$ in $\R^d$ under $\ell_1$ metric. (For details we refer to Section~\ref{sec:warm-up}.) Moreover, we assume for the time being that $q$ is contained in the bounding box of $c_1$ and $c_2$. We argue later how this assumption can be removed. A natural idea is to sample a small random subset $I$ of coordinates in a preprocessing and store $I$ as well as the projections (restrictions) $c_1^{(I)}$ and $c_2^{(I)}$ of $c_1$ and $c_2$ to $I$, respectively, as a data structure. For any query point $q$, we can then output index~$1$ (representing~$c_1$) if the projection $q^{(I)}$ is nearer to $c_1^{(I)}$ than to $c_2^{(I)}$ and output $2$ otherwise. To guarantee desirable concentration properties, our preprocessing uses importance sampling as follows. We sample each coordinate $b\in[d]$ with a probability $p^{(b)}$ that is proportional to the coordinate difference $|c_1^{(b)}-c_2^{(b)}|$, that is, $p^{(b)}=|c_1^{(b)}-c_2^{(b)}|/\|c_1-c_2\|_1$. We rescale each sampled coordinate $b$ by $1/p^{(b)}$ to achieve the following. First, the distances are preserved in expectation. Second, the coordinate differences are upper bounded, which achieves good concentration properties for large distances. (Here we are using the bounding box assumption.) This, in turn, allows us to prove the following \emph{comparator property}: Let $\epsilon, \delta\in (0,1)$ and assume we sample a set $I$ of $O(\log (1/\delta)/\epsilon^2)$ coordinates using importance sampling. If $\|c_2-q\|_1>(1+\epsilon)\|c_1-q\|_1$ then $\|c_2^{(I)}-q^{(I)}\|_1>(1+\epsilon/2)\|c_2^{(I)}-q^{(I)}\|$ with probability $1-\delta$.       
This data structure, which we call \emph{two-point comparator}, allows us to compare the distances of $q$ to $c_1$ and $c_2$. Moreover, it can be easily leveraged to a first sublinear data structure for $n$ points by storing a comparator for each pair of input points. Given any query point, we can then compute a $(1+\epsilon)$-approximate nearest neighbor using (a tweak of) classic minimum search that uses $n-1$ comparisons and therefore has only near-linear query complexity (number of coordinates by $q$ accessed by the query algorithm). The query algorithm selects the pairs of points it compares \emph{adaptively}, that is, based on the outcome of previous comparisons. This requires us to store a comparator for all point pairs in the preprocessing, that is, quadratically many coordinates overall.

\paragraph{Main Contribution: Near-Linear Space via Coordinated Sampling.}\joachim{add references to the various technical sections} To improve the space consumption, it may appear to be an obvious (and perhaps a typical computer science) attempt to reduce the number of comparisons the algorithm may potentially perform across all possible query points, for example, using the geometry of the input points. However, we do not know if this idea can indeed be used to obtain a subquadratic bound on the number of sampled coordinates. Instead, our main approach is the following: Rather than sampling coordinates for multiple pairs separately, we design a single, \emph{coordinated} sampling scheme capturing the comparators of all pairs simultaneously and allowing us to perform queries with near-linear complexity \emph{non-adaptively}. In a second step, we employ standard dimension reduction tools to turn this non-adaptive scheme into a data structure with near-linear space. Our coordinated sampling scheme works roughly as follows: Let $C=\{c_1,\dots,c_n\}$ be the set of input points. We (independently) sample each coordinate $b$ with a probability proportional to $\max_{ij}p^{(b)}_{ij}$ where $p_{ij}^{(b)}$ denotes the sampling probability of $b$ under the comparator for pair $c_i,c_j\in C$, that is, to the maximum sampling probability across all comparators. Indeed, this choice of probabilities ensures for any point pair $(c_i,c_j)$ that we sample each coordinate $b$ with probability at least $p_{ij}^{(b)}$ allowing us to  prove the comparator property for this pair.

The crucial question is: Why would this scheme reduce the non-adaptive query complexity compared to the overall quadratic number of coordinates sampled by the comparators? We sketch the proof idea for the special case where the input points and the query point come from the boolean cube~$\{0,1\}^d$.\danupon{This sentence is long. How about; For intuition, let's sassume for now that $c_i\in \{0,1\}^XXX$.} The overall number of coordinates accessed under the naive sampling scheme can be expressed as $R(C)=\sum_{ij}\sum_b p_{ij}^{(b)}$, which is bounded above by $n^2$ due to $\sum_b p_{ij}^{(b)}=\sum_b|c_i^{(b)}-c_j^{(b)}|/\|c_i-c_j\|_1=1$. The overall number of coordinates accessed by our coordinated sampling scheme is proportional to $S(C)=\sum_b p^{(b)}=\sum_b \max_{ij} p_{ij}^{(b)}$, which can be upper bounded by $\sum_{b}\sum_{ij} p_{ij}^{(b)}=R(C)$. To obtain an improved upper bound assume w.l.o.g.\ that $(c_1,c_2)$ is the closest pair in $C$. Notice that $p^{(b)}=p_{12}^{(b)}$ for every coordinate $b$ in the set $D$ of coordinates at which $c_1$ and $c_2$ differ because they minimize $\|c_i-c_j\|_1$ and hence maximize $p_{ij}^{(b)}$. Therefore $S(C)$ drops by at most $\sum_b p_{12}^{(b)}=1$ if we remove the coordinates in $D$ from all input points. On the other hand, we can identify $c_1$ and $c_2$ after removing $D$ obtaining a set $C'$ with at most $n-1$ points. Thus $S(C)\leq S(C')+1$. Using induction we can upper bound $S(C)$ by $n$ significantly improving upon the quadratic bound on $R(C)$. This inductive proof strategy can be extended to the general $\ell_1$ metric although the proof is notably more technical. Even though $\ell_1$ and $\ell_2$ metrics are equivalent on the boolean cube, we do not know how to extend the above proof strategy to the general $\ell_2$ metric. To obtain a linear bound for $\ell_2$ we rely on an entirely different, linear-algebra approach leveraging a latent connection between our sampling probabilities and the singular-value decomposition.\danupon{Write at most 2 sentences. IS this novel, standard, advanced, etc.? How does it compare to the literature (which papers)?}

While the above scheme gives near-linear non-adaptive query complexity (for $\ell_1$ and $\ell_2$) by sampling a set $I$ of linearly many coordinates a naive implementation still requires quadratic space: We would store for each of the~$n$ points in $C$ all coordinates in $I$. To obtain near-linear space we apply a standard dimension reduction technique such as the Johnson-Lindenstrauss transform for $\ell_2$. Specifically, we sample a suitable random $m\times |I|$ matrix $M$, where $m$ depends logarithmically on $n$, and compress $C$ to the $m$-dimensional point set $C'=\{\,Mc_i^{(I)}\mid c_i\in C \,\}$ requiring near-linear space. Storing $M$ itself requires near-linear space $m|I|$ space, too, as does storing $I$. Upon receiving a query point $q$ our data structure computes $Mq^{(I)}$ and outputs the index of the nearest neighbor in $C'$, which has near-linear query complexity.

\paragraph{Removing the Bounding Box Assumption.} Consider a query point $q$ that lies outside the bounding box of two points $c_i,c_j\in C$. Unfortunately, our importance sampling approach looses its strong concentration properties on the set of coordinates $b$ where $q^{(b)}$ lies outside the interval $[c_i^{(b)},c_j^{(b)}]$ and hence the comparator property may fail with too high probability. Specifically, it may happen that $\|q-c_j\|_1>(1+\epsilon)\|q-c_i\|_1$ and yet $\|q^{(I)}-c_j^{(I)}\|_1=(1+\delta)\|q^{(I)}-c_i^{(I)}\|_1$ for some positive $\delta\ll\epsilon$. In such a scenario, applying dimension reduction to the set $\{\,c^{(I)}\mid c\in C\,\}$ of projected points may invalidate the order of their distances to the query point.

A natural attempt to resolve this issue is to ``truncate'' the query point so it comes sufficiently close to the bounding box. This approach is described in Section~\ref{sec:point-reduction} and can in fact be applied to general $\ell_p$ metrics. However, it prevents us from applying dimension reduction because it requires explicitly storing the projected points and therefore takes quadratic space.

To retain our near-linear space bound, we do not actually change the sampling or the query procedure. Rather, we relax in our analysis on the requirement that the comparator property needs to hold for all point pairs. Specifically, we argue (using Markov) that with sufficiently high probability our distance estimate to the (unknown) nearest neighbor $o$ is within a bounded factor (*). We argue that under this condition all comparisons with $o$ do satisfy the comparator property, which is enough to satisfy correctness. Towards this, we distinguish for each $c\in C$ between \emph{good} coordinates $b$ where $q^{(b)}$ is relatively close to the interval $[c^{(b)},o^{(b)}]$. For the set of good coordinates, strong concentration properties hold---similar to those under the bounding box assumption. For the remaining (bad) coordinates, we use two properties: First, property (*) automatically implies that the total contribution of the bad coordinates cannot be too high. Second, the query point is indifferent between $c$ and $o$ on the set of bad coordinates as it is sufficiently distant and thus indifferent between $o$ and $c$ on these coordinates. Together, these properties imply that (a relaxation of) the comparator property holds for all comparisons with $o$ thereby implying the $(1+\epsilon)$-approximation guarantee also after removing the bounding box assumption. For details, we refer to the full technical sections below.

