\begin{theorem}\label{theorem:sample-reuse-l1}
    Consider a set $C$ of $n$ points $c_1, \dots, c_n \in \R^d$ equipped with $\ell_1$ metric. Given $0 < \epsilon < 1/4$ and $0 < \delta < 1$, with probability $(1-\delta)$, we can efficiently construct a randomized data structure (See \cref{alg:sample-reuse-l1}) such that for any query point $q\in \R^d$ the following conditions hold with probability $1-\delta$. (i) The data structure reads $O(n \log (n/\delta)/(\epsilon^3\delta^2))$ coordinates $q$. (ii) It returns $i\in [d]$ where $c_i$ is a $(1+\epsilon)$-approximate nearest neighbor of $q$ in $C$. (iii) The data structure has a size of $O(n \log^2(n/\delta) \log(d)/(\epsilon^5\delta^4))$ words. 
