Creating ontologies from databases has required significant manual effort by domain experts or the use of semi-automated tools. These approaches rely on database schema (e.g., table and column names) only and lack access to external knowledge. As a result, they produce basic ontologies that fail to align with existing vocabularies or human-defined semantics \cite{ben2021novel}. Consequently, the resulting knowledge graphs may syntactically represent the data but lack the representative vocabulary and precise axiomatic definitions required for effective use by humans and machine reasoners.

Large language models (LLMs) \cite{zhao2023llsurvey} have recently shown a remarkable ability to comprehend text, capture and encode conceptual knowledge \cite{xiong2025tokens}, perform algorithmic reasoning \cite{zhou2022teaching}, and generate structured outputs by following instructions \cite{liu2024llms,liu2024we}. 
Models such as OpenAI’s ChatGPT (GPT-4) \cite{roumeliotis2023chatgpt} and open-source models like Meta AI’s LLaMA 3 \cite{grattafiori2024llama} and DeepSeek-V3 \cite{guo2025deepseek} 
%https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.12948
have shown near-human performance on many knowledge-intensive tasks. LLMs have recently been used for ontology generation from text \cite{babaei2023llms4olllmonto,qiang2023agentllmonto,mihindukulasooriya2023text2kgbenchllmonto,norouzi2025ontology,lippolis2025ontologyllmonto}, but to the best of our knowledge, no work has yet explored the use of LLMs for ontology generation from relational databases.

