\subsection{Ontology Extraction from Relational Databases.}
The semantic web and data integration communities have a long history of converting relational data to semantic representations \cite{DBLP:journals/ws/VolzHSSS04,kohler2000logical,meersman2001ontologies,astrova2004reverse}. 
%\steffen{It seems to me that \cite{lyngbaek1987mapping} goes the other direction, i.e. Extended ER to rel DB. Also I added our own work from 2004}
The \textit{relational-to-ontology mapping} problem was formalized in early work with the goal of developing an ontology that faithfully reflects the database content \cite{astrova2004reverse}. 

The W3C Direct Mapping and R2RML standards treat each table row as an RDF resource, providing a baseline knowledge graph that mirrors the physical schema and lacks higher-level abstraction or domain alignment \cite{directmapsequeda2012directly,dirmap1arenas2012direct,world2012r2rml}.  
BootOX automates relational-to-OWL mappings (table $\rightarrow$ class, foreign key $\rightarrow$ object property, etc.) and can invoke LogMap to align the resulting ontology with a chosen domain vocabulary, thereby accelerating ontology-based data-access solutions \cite{jimenez2015bootox}.

% The W3C Direct Mapping \cite{directmapsequeda2012directly,dirmap1arenas2012direct} and R2RML \cite{world2012r2rml} standards defined how each table row can be seen as an RDF resource with properties for each column, providing a baseline for materializing a knowledge graph from a database.
% However, direct mappings result in ontologies that lack any higher-level abstraction or alignment to domain vocabularies, instead just mirroring the physical schema (e.g., table name to class, column name to property).
% Systems such as BootOX \cite{jimenez2015bootox} generate relational database to OWL ontology mappings automatically, speeding up the development of ontology-based data access systems.
% They employ the direct mapping approach, in which each table becomes an OWL class, each foreign key an object property, etc., and optionally employ ontology mapping (through the LogMap system) to align the generated ontology with a specified domain ontology.

Other tools and approaches (e.g., Karma \cite{karmaknoblock2012semi},
IncMap \cite{incmappinkel2017incmap}, MIRROR \cite{de2015mirror}, 
D2RQ \cite{d2rqvergoulis2014data}) have been evaluated in a benchmark for automatic relational-to-ontology mapping generation called RODI \cite{rodipinkel2017rodi}.
%https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:5261ded8-e61e-44cd-8139-d3c629637185/files/me113edfdbc1b3361c415a1dc6b793296#page=4.17
The RODI benchmark \cite{rodipinkel2017rodi} evaluates how well systems like BootOX generate mappings that support correct query answering across domains (e.g., conference, geography, oil and gas). While effective for simple cases, results showed that complex scenarios with significant schema-ontology mismatches remain challenging.
% To assess how well systems like BootOX can generate mappings that allow for the correct answer of the queries, the RODI benchmark presents several domains (conference, geography, and oil and gas) with a target ontology and queries for each. 
% Results from RODI showed that while some systems can handle simple scenarios,
% the more complex integration cases (where the database schema and target ontology differ significantly in structure or naming) remained challenging \cite{rodipinkel2017rodi}.

Unlike prior works based on heuristics or string matching, our approach uses RAG-LLMs for semantic understanding and reasoning \cite{xiong2025tokens,zhou2022teaching} to bridge the gap between database schemas and ontologies via contextualized semantic mapping.
% In contrast to previous systems that relied on fixed heuristics or string matching, 
% our work comprehends and bridges the semantic gap between the database and the ontology by using LLMs (with internal conceptual knowledge \cite{xiong2025tokens} and algorithmic reasoning \cite{zhou2022teaching}) with RAG (capturing similarity in semantic space using external sources) as the schema mapping generator.

\textbf{Ontology learning} from databases using machine learning or additional metadata has been the subject of more recent studies \cite{ma2022ontologyfromdatabase}. 
For example, \cite{ben2021novel} suggests a technique for learning ontologies from RDBs that uses metrics for factual (ABox) and conceptual (TBox) quality to assess the approach. It introduces new heuristics to extract taxonomy and part-whole relations from the analysis of foreign keys and data patterns.
They and others emphasize how crucial it is to assess the generated ontologies' instance correctness and schema coverage. These efforts have a number of shortcomings, including
a) relying on a static predefined mapping rule, which restricts generalization; 
b) assuming pre-cleared and normalized data while disregarding unstructured data; and 
c) lacking alignment with external ontologies or reuse. 
We refer to these surveys \cite{passant2012bringingsurvey,motik2007bridging,passant2012bringingsurvey,ma2022ontologyfromdatabase}
for a comprehensive overview of other related works.


