\subsection{Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG).}
% The idea of enhancing generative models with retrieval was brought to attention \cite{raglewis2020retrieval} in the context of open-domain question answering. 
% Their RAG model could produce more factual and up-to-date responses than a stand-alone parametric model by retrieving relevant text passages from Wikipedia.
% Further studies have shown that offering relevant context can guide LLMs and lower hallucinations by using RAG on tasks including long-form question answering, conversation, and even code generation \cite{raggao2023retrieval}.
The idea of enhancing generative models with retrieval gained attention in open-domain QA, where RAG \cite{raglewis2020retrieval} improved factual accuracy by retrieving relevant Wikipedia passages. Later works \cite{raggao2023retrieval} showed that providing relevant context via RAG reduces hallucinations in tasks like long-form QA, conversation, and code generation.
RAG approaches can be classified into several types \cite{raggao2023retrieval}: \textit{naive RAG} (simple similarity-based retrieval added to prompts), 
\textit{advanced RAG} (with query rewriting and reranking), \textit{iterative RAG} (multi-step retrieval and selection), \textit{recursive RAG} (breaking queries into sub-problems for iterative retrieval and generation), and \textit{adaptive RAG} (dynamically switching between iterative and recursive strategies).
% RAG approaches can be categorized in several ways \cite{raggao2023retrieval}: naive RAG (retrieving documents via similarity search and adding them to the prompt), advanced RAG (involving pre- and post-retrieval processes like query rewriting and reranking), iterative RAG (performing retrieval over multiple iterations and selecting the best results), recursive RAG (refining the user query into sub-problems, solved iteratively through retrieval and generation), and adaptive RAG (dynamically choosing between iterative and recursive methods).
In addition, RAG approaches can also be categorized based on the data type, such as text-based RAG, GraphRAG, multimodal RAG, hybrid RAG (combining different types of input data), and structured data RAG (retrieving and generating based on structured data sources, such as databases or tables).
In our work, we choose an \textbf{advanced hybrid recursive RAG}.

% In our work, we use retrieval in a novel way: rather than retrieving from a large corpus like Wikipedia, we retrieve pieces of the \emph{database schema and ontology} that are relevant to the current subtasks of KG construction. For instance, when generating a class for a particular table, we retrieve that table's description and any related tables' names or the core ontology classes that might match. This provides the LLM with a focused view, similar to how a human would concentrate on one part of the design at a time. We also retrieve examples from the core ontology or previously generated parts of the KG to maintain consistency in style and naming. Our experiments (Section~\ref{sec:results}) confirm that this retrieval-guided prompting leads to better results than prompting the LLM with the entire schema at once or not including ontology context.

