\subsection{Competency Question Generation}
Competency Questions (CQs) are natural language queries used to verify if an ontology captures the required domain knowledge \cite{gruninger1995methodology}. Traditionally, formulating CQs has been manual and labor-intensive, supported by templates and controlled language patterns that still require significant expert effort \cite{DBLP:conf/mtsr/KeetMA19}.
Recently, LLMs have been used to automate CQ generation from ontology triples \cite{DBLP:conf/sac/AlharbiTGP24,DBLP:conf/esws/RebboudTLT24}, domain texts \cite{DBLP:conf/kgswc/AntiaK23}, and knowledge graphs \cite{DBLP:journals/ws/CirokuBKMPS24}, showing promising results in producing contextually relevant CQs. Building on recent LLM-as-judge approaches \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2411-15594,gu2024surveyllmjudge}, we use LLMs both to generate CQs and to evaluate whether the generated ontology meets these requirements. This automated evaluation helps verify consistency, coverage, and completeness, serving as a practical alternative to exhaustive human review.
