\subsection{Results}
Upon inspecting the generated outputs, baseline and non-iterative methods often failed to produce valid ontologies—frequently yielding invalid Turtle syntax or irrelevant outputs (e.g., SQL). As a result, it was not possible to merge delta ontologies or evaluate them using structural, logical, or semantic strategies. Consequently, Strategies \textbf{1–5} report results only for \textbf{RIGOR}-generated outputs. In contrast, Strategy \textbf{6} (Ontology Quality through CQ Performance) was applied to all generation methods, as it evaluates each delta ontology independently without requiring ontology merging or syntactic validity.

\textbf{1. Syntax Validity Check:} All \textbf{RIGOR}-generated ontologies were parsed in Protégé and conformed to the OWL 2 DL profile, ensuring syntactic validity.

\textbf{2. Logical Consistency Check:}  All \textbf{RIGOR}-generated ontologies were deemed consistent by the HermiT reasoner.

\textbf{3. Criteria-Based Evaluation (Modeling Pitfalls): } Table~\ref{tab:pitfalls} presents only \textit{critical} and \textit{important} modeling pitfalls as categorized by OOPS!, excluding low-severity pitfalls such as missing annotations. Overall, the ontologies demonstrated sound modeling practices. The most frequent issue was (\texttt{P19}) (Multiple domains or ranges), which may arise in auto-generated ontologies when property reuse is not well defined in the prompt. A few cases of (\texttt{P34}) (Untyped class) and (\texttt{P12}/\texttt{P30}) (missing equivalence declarations) were detected, but these reflect refinement opportunities rather than structural errors. The absence of critical pitfalls—such as defining wrong relations (\texttt{P05, P27, P28, P29, P31})  or cycles in hierarchies (\texttt{P06})—indicates that the generated ontologies adhere to core principles of formal ontology modeling.

\textbf{4. Structural Analysis:} We compared structural metrics across ontologies generated by different \textit{Gen-LLMs} under the \textbf{RIGOR} framework. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:structural_analysis}, DeepSeek consistently produced the most structurally rich ontologies, with the highest counts of classes and axioms. Mistral generated the smallest ontologies in terms of class count, though it occasionally had high data property counts, especially in the ICU domain. 
Overall, Ontologies from the ICU database were structurally more complex than those from the real-world database, indicating a correlation between database size and the number of classes, properties, and axioms, consistent with the ICU database's larger schema in Section~\ref{RD}.
% Overall, ontologies derived from the ICU database were structurally more complex than those from the real-world database, suggesting a correlation between database size and the number of ontology classes, properties, and axioms—consistent with the larger schema of the ICU database described in Section~\ref{RD}.



\textbf{5. Semantic Coverage of Database Schema: } This analysis was performed on the \texttt{chemotherapy} ontology—one of the delta ontologies generated using the \textbf{RIGOR} framework and LLaMA 3.1 using the real-world database. In total, 21 out of 29 ontology classes were matched to column names, yielding a \textit{class coverage} of 72.4\%, indicating strong semantic alignment and showing how well the local features of the database table are represented in the corresponding delta ontology. Figure~\ref{fig:sidebyside} visualizes the \texttt{chemotherapy} ontology and shows the heatmap of class-to-column alignment. Unmatched table columns (e.g., \texttt{patient\_id}) refer to concepts modeled in other delta ontologies within the full ontology; for instance, \texttt{patient\_id} appears in the \texttt{patient} ontology and is omitted from the \texttt{chemotherapy} ontology by the LLM to avoid redundancy. Unmatched ontology classes (e.g., \texttt{TreatmentPlan}) were derived from external ontology repositories or retrieved documentation as part of our RAG pipeline.  

\textbf{6. Ontology Quality through CQ Performance: } Table~\ref{tab2} shows that the RIGOR method consistently outperformed Baseline and Non-Iterative approaches across all models and databases. Detailed scores across all dimensions are reported in Tables~\ref{judge_llm_results1}–\ref{judge_llm_results2}. The highest CQ scores were achieved by DeepSeek 67B on the real-world database (4.6 ± 0.11) and Llama 3.1 on ICU (4.6 ± 0.12). Mistral 3.1 performed slightly lower but still benefitted from RIGOR. 
