\subsection{Phase 1: Selection of Best Prompts and Intermediate Representations}

\begin{figure*}[ht!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/representations-2.png}
    \caption{Sample code snippet with its AST and NL summary.}
  \label{fig:representations}
\end{figure*}

In Phase 1, we investigate LLM-driven code translation with various ways of including intermediate representations.  As an example, Figure~\ref{fig:representations} shows a piece of code and its corresponding AST and NL representations. The AST shows the structure of the code snippet in a tree-style format and is derived by using the `ast' method in Python. Notably, the AST must be flattened (converted to text) for input to an LLM. The NL summary describes the code in plain text. 
%The Rosetta Code website contains 1,310 code snippets and their corresponding descriptions \cite{rosettacode}.

In this phase, we evaluate the performance of the following options using the HuggingFace version of the open-source GPT-4 as the backbone (Open Gpt4 8X7B~\cite{theblokeopengpt4}), with Appendix~\ref{appendix:tested-prompt-variations} showing example prompts for each. 

\begin{itemize}

\item Zero-shot prompt (0SP), corresponding to previous work on code translation \cite{pan2024lost}.

\item One-shot prompt (1SP), with one example of source-to-target language translation for each source-target pair, randomly selected from the CodeTransOcean dataset~\cite{yan-etal-2023-codetransocean}.

\item A zero-shot two-step approach of having the LLM translate the source language to either AST (2S-AST), NL (2S-NL), both AST and NL (2S-AST-NL), or both NL and AST (2S-NL-AST), followed by having the LLM translate the intermediate representation to the target language.  

\item A one-shot CoT prompt in which the LLM is instructed to use either AST (CoT-AST), NL (CoT-NL), both AST and NL (CoT-AST-NL), or both NL and AST (CoT-NL-AST) to explain its reasoning. The AST was obtained using the Python `ast' method whereas the NL was obtained from the Rosetta Code website~\cite{rosettacode}. The same example used in the 1SP was also used here.
\end{itemize}

%using a zero-shot prompt (0SP), a one-shot prompt (1SP), a two-step prompt with an intermediate NL summary (2S-NL), a two-step prompt with an intermediate AST translation (2S-AST), a two-step prompt with an AST  followed by a NL summary as the intermediate step (2S-AST-NL), a two-step prompt with a NL summary followed by an AST as the intermediate step (2S-NL-AST), a chain-of-thought prompt with an intermediate NL summary (CoT-NL), a chain-of-thought prompt with an intermediate AST translation (CoT-AST), a chain-of-thought prompt with an AST and a NL summary in the intermediate step (CoT-AST-NL), and a chain-of-thought prompt with a NL summary and an AST in the intermediate step (CoT-NL-AST). 
For the combination of AST and NL, we test different orders given that prior work highlighted the importance of order in prompting~\cite{kumar2021reordering}. 

%We utilize the prompts presented by Pan et al. for the zero-shot prompt type~\cite{pan2024lost} which we also used as our baseline and iterated on it for the other prompt variations. Given the best performance was achieved by GPT-4, we considered this LLM model for our study. However, we leveraged the open-source GPT-4 model as a proxy for the restricted GPT-4 model offered by ChatGPT for replication and access purposes. The same open-source version of the GPT-4 model from Hugging Face~\cite{theblokeopengpt4} was used with all prompts. Similar to Pan et al.~\cite{pan2024lost}, we also evaluated with the percentage of successful translations to compare the performance of the prompts. Notably the examples used in the prompts were obtained from the CodeTransOcean dataset~\cite{yan-etal-2023-codetransocean}. Samples from a separate dataset were used to avoid using something that is in the evaluated dataset. Arguably, even if this example does occur in the evaluated dataset, it is only one sample so the impact would be negligible.  

\subsection{Phase 2: Testing Generalizability with New Dataset and New LLMs}

\textbf{New Dataset.} After comparing the prompts using Open Gpt4 8X7B on the CodeNet dataset in Phase 1, we gauge the generalizability of our findings with another dataset in Phase 2A. We use the AVATAR dataset here~\cite{ahmad2023avatarparallelcorpusjavapython} due to its large size and the availability of tests to allow for the computation of the percentage of successful translation metric. Other datasets such as CodeTransOcean~\cite{yan-etal-2023-codetransocean} and CodeTrans from CodeXGLUE~\cite{lu2021codexgluemachinelearningbenchmark} do not include tests that could be used for judging whether the translation was successful and instead rely on metrics such as exact match, BLEU~\cite{papineni2002bleu}, and CodeBLEU~\cite{ren2020codebleu}, techniques that compare token n-grams in the generated text with the original reference.  These metrics may be inappropriate for code translation: high n-gram similarity does not necessarily imply code correctness in the presence of subtle bugs and low n-gram similarity might be due to a different way to write a correct program \cite{pan2024lost}. 
%using LLMs given past research showing that errors due to compilation and runtime are present in code generated by LLMs \cite{chen2021evaluating,ahmad2023avatarparallelcorpusjavapython}.
%and our research goal to enhance the code translation task by producing output that is compilable, executable, and correct.

\textbf{New Models.} Finally, in Phase 2B, we investigate how the two best prompts from Phase 1 compare against the baseline prompt (zero-shot) for specialized open-source LLMs. We chose StarCoder~\cite{li2023starcodersourceyou} and CodeGen~\cite{nijkamp2023codegenopenlargelanguage} since they had the best code translation performance (without considering intermediate representations) in \cite{pan2024lost}. CodeGeeX was another potential option, but it was no longer available on their website and the authors did not respond to email inquiries about model availability. We run these three prompts (zero-shot, top two best prompts from Open Gpt4 8X7B on CodeNet) for these LLM models (StarCoder, and CodeGen) on both the CodeNet~\cite{puricodenet} and AVATAR ~\cite{ahmad2023avatarparallelcorpusjavapython} datasets to investigate the transferability of the outcomes. 
%Similar to previous experiments, we leveraged the zero-shot prompt from Pan et al.~\cite{pan2024lost} as our baseline prompt for comparison.

