\begin{abstract}
Deep hiding has been exploring the hiding capability of deep learning-based models, aiming to conceal image-level messages into cover images and reveal them from generated stego images. Existing schemes are easily detected by steganalyzers due to their large payloads and their limitation to feature extraction based solely on either pure convolution or pure transformer operators within a single range, as well as pixel-level loss constraints. To address the issue, in this paper, we introduce generation-based adversarial attacks into color JPEG image deep hiding and propose a \underline{m}ulti-range \underline{r}epresentations-driven \underline{a}dversarial stego \underline{g}eneration framework called MRAG from a steganalysis perspective. Specifically, we integrate the local-range neighbor reception characteristic of the convolution and the global-range dependency modeling of the transformer to construct MRAG. Meanwhile, we use the transformed images obtained through coarse-grained and fine-grained frequency decomposition as inputs, introducing multi-grained information. Furthermore, a features angle-norm disentanglement loss is designed to constrain the generated stegos closer to covers in the angle and norm space of the steganalyzer’s classified features. Consequently, small yet effective adversarial perturbations can be injected into the process of generating stegos, ensuring that stegos maintain favorable secret restorability and imperceptibility. Extensive experiments demonstrate that MRAG can achieve state-of-the-art performance.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\begin{figure}[t]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=1.0\columnwidth]{Fig1} % Reduce the figure size so that it is slightly narrower than the column. Don't use precise values for figure width.This setup will avoid overfull boxes.
	\caption{The illustration shows the performance distinctions between MRAG and existing deep hiding methods when the cover is a color JPEG image. (a) RIIS \protect\cite{Xu:22}, a robust DH-Spatial method, produces visible artifacts in the JPEG stego and the restored secret, resulting in poor visual imperceptibility and restorability. (b) Advanced EFDR \protect\cite{Yangb:23} has acceptable visual effects but is easily detected by steganalyzers. (c) The proposed MRAG can produce a JPEG stego with satisfying secret restorability, visual imperceptibility, and steganalysis imperceptibility.}
	\label{fig1}
\end{figure}
Image steganography is the art and science of sheltering confidential information in publicly available cover images to obtain stego ones, from which secret information can be revealed, and resulting stegos should be as unsuspicious as possible \cite{Provos:03}. It usually has three primary metrics \cite{Pevny:08}: the capacity refers to embedded payloads, the visual imperceptibility regarding the cover and stego being indistinguishable by naked eyes, and the steganalysis imperceptibility against the steganalyzers. Traditional steganographic methods and earlier deep learning-based methods maintain the bit-level payloads, typically setting it below 0.4 bits per pixel (BPP), with traditional methods going up to 1 BPP at most, and deep learning-based methods reaching up to 4.5 BPP \cite{Lu:21}. In light of this, Baluja \cite{Baluja:17} delves deeper into the potential of steganographic models based on deep learning, successfully hiding a spatial RGB image into another one of the same size, known as deep hiding. Three natural advantages of deep hiding are also pointed out: 1) Since the type of hidden secret information is an image with high inherent correlations, the difficulty of network learning can be appropriately reduced. 2) The secret itself has a certain degree of fault tolerance. 3) The magnitude of the payload can be up to 24 BPP with a 1:1 ratio between the hidden secret image and the cover image. The success of the pioneering work, along with three natural advantages, paves the way for the flourishing of deep hiding.

The act of hiding inevitably yields embedded artifacts within the stegos. Whether artifacts are discovered usually depends on three factors \cite{Baluja:20}: the hidden capacity, the imperceptibility of the algorithm, and the texture of the image itself. The likelihood of the hiding behavior being discovered is negatively correlated with the latter two factors, and positively correlated with the capacity. In this sense, the capacity advantage of deep hiding also makes it more susceptible to detection, turning it into a disadvantage. Deep hiding with spatial RGB images as covers, abbreviated as DH-Spatial, has made significant progress recently \cite{Zhang:20,Lu:21,Xu:22}. However, the latest work, EFDR \cite{Yangb:23}, points out that DH-Spatial methods perform poorly when the cover is the ubiquitous lossy image format - color JPEG image, resulting in visible color distortion, blocking, blurring, and banding artifacts in the generated JPEG stego. Similar visible artifacts also appear in the restored secret revealed from the JPEG stego, as shown in Fig.\ref{fig1} (a). Although EFDR achieves acceptable visual imperceptibility and restorability, its steganalysis imperceptibility remains unsatisfactory, as depicted in Fig.\ref{fig1} (b). The common shortcomings of these schemes are that they rely solely on single-range feature extraction using either pure convolution or pure transformer operators, and only consider pixel-level loss constraints.

A steganalyzer is a binary classifier that distinguishes between cover and stego images. With the development of deep learning, the way of features extraction has changed from manual to automatic extraction by networks and has become mainstream \cite{Wei:24,Hu:24}. Deep learning-based classifiers are found to be vulnerable to adversarial examples that can cause misclassification, which brings the prospect of resisting steganalyzers based on deep learning \cite{Zhangb:18}. Reference \cite{Szegedy:14} puts forward that the adversarial example can be viewed as “accidental steganography”. They are achieved by adding low amplitude signals to an image for adversarial attacks or secret embedding and extraction. Analogously, deep hiding and generation-based adversarial attacks employ an image-to-image generative architecture to learn the image transformation task from the input image to the generated image \cite{Liu:19}. Therefore, in this paper, we attempt to introduce generation-based adversarial attacks into color JPEG image deep hiding, exploring the potential of generated color JPEG stego images in terms of secret restorability and imperceptibility, as illustrated in Fig.\ref{fig1} (c).

For the steganalyzer, to better capture subtle changes, it is essential to consider both local-range and global-range information \cite{Wei:24}. This in turn inspires us to integrate the local-range neighbor reception characteristic of convolution and the global-range dependency modeling of transformer, to construct a \underline{m}ulti-range \underline{r}epresentations-driven \underline{a}dversarial stego \underline{g}eneration framework called MRAG from a steganalysis perspective. Specifically, MRAG comprises four key components: the invertible local-range branch, the invertible global-range branch, the adaptive fusion module, and the surrogate steganalyzer. We also design the coarse-grained frequency decomposition (CFD) and the fine-grained frequency decomposition (FFD) to transform the input. According to the dimension characteristic of the obtained transformed images, the transformed images are used as the input for the local and global branches, respectively, naturally introducing multi-grained frequency information. Moreover, a features angle-norm disentanglement loss is designed to disentangle the surrogate steganalyzer’s classified features into the angle space associated with inter-class semantic differences and the norm space related to intra-class confidence, respectively. It can constrain the generated stegos closer to covers by pulling together the angle between the classified features of cover and stego, while keeping the norm changeless. With the help of features angle-norm disentanglement loss, small yet effective adversarial perturbations can be injected based on the multi-range representations extracted by local and global branches, ensuring that stegos maintain favorable secret restorability and imperceptibility. Our contributions can be summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}
	\item We propose a multi-range representations-driven adversarial stego generation framework, MRAG, to generate color JPEG stego images with favorable secret restorability and imperceptibility. To our knowledge, this is the first to focus on the steganalysis imperceptibility issue of color JPEG image deep hiding. 
	\item We integrate the local-range neighbor reception of the convolution and the global-range dependency modeling of the transformer to extract multi-range representations from a steganalysis perspective, injecting adversarial perturbations based on these representations.
	\item A features angle-norm disentanglement loss is designed to constrain the angle and norm distances between the steganalyzer's classified features of the cover and the stego, in which the stego can be closer to the cover.	
	\item Experiments validate the effectiveness of MRAG, demonstrating that MRAG establishes a new state-of-the-art benchmark in color JPEG image deep hiding.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}

In this section, we summarize advancements in deep hiding and briefly introduce generation-based adversarial attacks.

\subsection{Deep hiding} 

Seminal work \cite{Baluja:17} designs a deep hiding model that contains a secret pre-processing module, a hiding module, and a revealing module. UDH \cite{Zhang:20} is a cover-agnostic deep hiding model, which discards the pre-processing structure and only encodes secret images in the hiding sub-network. ISN \cite{Lu:21} treats embedding and extraction as an inverse problem and utilizes the reversibility of invertible neural networks to address this question through its forward and backward processes. HiNet \cite{Jing:21} combines invertible neural networks with the wavelet domain and introduces low-frequency wavelet loss. RIIS \cite{Xu:22}, a robust deep hiding model, takes various distortions into consideration, which shows acceptable robustness. The cover types and operators involved in the aforementioned works are always spatial RGB images and pure convolution. EFDR \cite{Yangb:23} is the first to research hiding image-level information in color JPEG images, focusing on mining fine-grained DCT representations using the pure transformer operator. Although advances in deep hiding have been remarkable, existing schemes are limited to single-range feature extraction, relying solely on either pure convolution or pure transformer operators. Moreover, these schemes are constrained only by pixel-level loss, leading to poor imperceptibility. It should be noted that, in this paper, we also perform deep hiding in color JPEG images, the ubiquitous lossy format images in daily life, and pay attention to its steganalysis imperceptibility issue first.

\subsection{Generation-based adversarial attacks} 

Reference \cite{Szegedy:14} unveils that deep learning-based classifiers are susceptible to adversarial examples. Adding tiny perturbations to the input can cause misclassification with high confidence. Adversarial attacks can mainly be divided into two categories: gradient-based attacks and generation-based attacks \cite{Macas:24}. Generation-based attacks usually utilize an image-to-image generative architecture to learn the mapping from input images to adversarial ones. Once the adversarial generator is trained well, only one forward propagation is needed to produce plenty of adversarial examples quickly. For example, AdvGAN \cite{Xiao:18}, a representative of generation-based attacks, consists of an adversarial generator, a discriminator that differentiates between the original and the adversarial example, and a surrogate classifier intended to be attacked. However, SSAE \cite{Lub:21} demonstrates that the discriminator is redundant if perturbations are tiny enough. Therefore, our MRAG also discards the discriminator to simplify the model.

\section{Proposed Framework}

\subsection{Overview}
\begin{figure*}[t]
	\centering
	{\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Fig2.pdf}}
	\caption{Overall architecture. In the MRAG's forward-hiding process, the spatial RGB secret image $S_{i}$ is hidden in the color JPEG cover image $C_{i}$ to obtain the JPEG stego image $S_{o}$ and the redundant information $R_{o}$, an all-zero matrix. In the MRAG's backward-revealing process, $S_{o}$ and $R_{o}$ are fed to reveal the secret image. Features angle-norm disentanglement disentangles the classified features learned by surrogate steganalyzer with the angle space and the norm space related to inter-class semantic differences and intra-class confidence, respectively. For effectively fooling surrogate steganalyzer, we pulls together the angles between the classified features of cover $\textbf{S}^{2}(C_{i})$ and the classified features of stego $\textbf{S}^{2}(S_{o})$ while keeping their norm values changeless. }
	\label{fig2}
\end{figure*}

The pipeline of MRAG consists of four key components: the invertible local-range branch, the invertible global-range branch, the adaptive fusion module, and the surrogate steganalyzer, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig2}.  Since we construct MRAG utilizing invertible neural networks as much as possible to ensure reversibility, it can implement forward-hiding and backward-revealing processes with shared parameters. The other image conversion operations and network structures are also reversible and symmetric. Conversion operations contain the coarse-grained frequency decomposition (CFD), the fine-grained frequency decomposition (FFD), and the inverse FFD (IFFD). The input of MRAG's forward-hiding process is the color JPEG cover image $C_{i}$ and the spatial RGB secret image $S_{i}$. The input is converted into coarse-grained and fine-grained frequency maps through CFD and CFD$\to$FFD, respectively. According to the dimension characteristic of the obtained frequency maps, they are fed into the local and global branches' forward processes, respectively. Then, the global branch's output passes through the IFFD and is concatenated with the local branch's output. Afterward, their concatenation is fed into the adaptive fusion module's forward process to obtain the JPEG stego image $S_{o}$ and the redundant information $R_{o}$, an all-zero matrix. MRAG's backward-revealing process takes the color JPEG stego image $S_{o}$ and $R_{o}$ as input to reveal the secret image. For effectively inducing the surrogate steganalyzer to misclassify the cover $C_{i}$ and stego $S_{o}$, we conduct the features angle-norm disentanglement and pull together the angles between the classified features of cover $\textbf{S}^{2}(C_{i})$ and the classified features of stego $\textbf{S}^{2}(S_{o})$ while keeping their norm values changeless. 

\subsection{MRAG Model}

\paragraph{Backbone structure.} Given that invertible neural networks can preserve as much detail as possible due to the theoretically lossless information characteristic \cite{Dinh:14,Dinh:17,Gilbert:17}, we utilize invertible neural networks to construct our MRAG to the greatest extent. As shown in Fig. \ref{fig2}, several key components of MRAG, including the invertible local-range branch, the invertible global-range branch, and the adaptive fusion module, all employ invertible neural networks. For the basic unit of invertible neural networks, the affine coupling layer, the input needs to be divided into two parts, $u_{i}$ and $l_{i}$, along the channel axis. Following reference \cite{Xiao:20}, we use an additive affine transformation for the upper part, $u_{i}$, and employ an enhanced affine transformation for the lower part, $l_{i}$. To be specific, the forward process of the affine coupling layer can be formulated as
\begin{equation}
	\begin{gathered}	
		u_{o}=u_{i}+\phi(l_{i}) \\
		l_{o}=l_{i}\odot{\textbf{exp}(\rho(u_{o}))}+\eta(u_{o})
		\label{eq1}
	\end{gathered}
\end{equation}
In Eq. (\ref{eq1}), $\phi(\bigcdot)$, $\rho(\bigcdot)$, and $\eta(\bigcdot)$ can be arbitrary network structures. $\odot$ and $\textbf{exp}(\bigcdot)$ are the Hadamard product and Exponential function, respectively. Note that $\textbf{exp}(\bigcdot)$ is omitted in Fig. \ref{fig2}. Correspondingly, the inverse process is
\begin{equation}
	\begin{gathered}
		l^{\prime}_{i}=(l^{\prime}_{o}-\eta(u^{\prime}_{o}))\odot\textbf{exp}(-\rho(u^{\prime}_{o})) \\
		u^{\prime}_{i}=u^{\prime}_{o}-\phi(l^{\prime}_{i})
		\label{eq2}
	\end{gathered}
\end{equation}

In the following, we will provide a detailed description of the four key components of MRAG. 

\paragraph{Invertible local-range branch.} Convolution performs excellently in image-related tasks and has nice local neighbor reception characteristics \cite{Krizhevsky:12}. Here, $\phi(\bigcdot)$, $\rho(\bigcdot)$, and $\eta(\bigcdot)$ in the invertible local-range branch is implemented by a 5-layer dense block with a $3\times3$ convolution kernel \cite{Huang:17}. In the dense block, the output feature maps of each layer are passed to all subsequent layers, greatly enhancing the ability for representation sharing and learning. For the invertible local-range branch, the input is coarse-grained frequency maps through CFD. CFD includes the Huffman decoding of the color JPEG cover image and the DCT transformation of the spatial RGB secret image.

\paragraph{Invertible global-range branch.} To further enhance representation and global dependency modeling ability, in the invertible global-range branch, $\phi(\bigcdot)$, $\rho(\bigcdot)$, and $\eta(\bigcdot)$ adopt a standard 1-layer Pre-LN transformer \cite{Dosovitskiy:21}. This Pre-LN transformer applies the layer normalization before the multi-head self-attention and the multi-layer perceptron for a more stable gradient distribution. For the invertible global-range branch, the input is the fine-grained frequency maps obtained through CFD$\to$FFD. FFD includes the sub-band gathering and the zigzag scanning of the coarse-grained frequency maps. The sub-band gathering procedure rearranges coarse-grained frequency maps to place the frequency components of the same sub-band on the same channel, making frequency components more compact. The zigzag scanning procedure further rearranges them along the channel axis to group similar frequency sub-bands together, exploiting the structural information in the JPEG transformation.

\paragraph{Adaptive fusion module.} Our designed adaptive fusion module consists of a pair of $1\times1$ convolutions with channel attention and a series of affine coupling layers with an invertible $1\times1$ convolution.  $\phi(\bigcdot)$, $\rho(\bigcdot)$, and $\eta(\bigcdot)$ in the adaptive fusion module also employ a 5-layer dense block. The $1\times1$ convolution with channel attention designed in reference \cite{Tan:22} is used to adaptively extract the most informative frequency representations. The invertible $1\times1$ convolution is a kind of convolution commonly used for invertible neural networks. Adding an invertible $1\times1$ convolution before each affine coupling layer can increase the dependencies between representations, allowing for more effective information fusion. For the adaptive fusion module, the input is the concatenation of representations extracted from the invertible local-range branch and representations extracted from the invertible global-range branch after undergoing IFFD. IFFD is used for dimension alignment.

\paragraph{Surrogate steganalyzer.} In our MRAG, the surrogate steganalyzer can be any well-trained JPEG steganalyzer. Here, we select three open-source steganalyzers: UCNet \cite{Wei:22}, EWNet \cite{Su:21}, and DBS2Net \cite{Hu:24}. UCNet is the sole steganalyzer tailored for color JPEG images. The others are straightforwardly based on existing steganalyzers for gray-scale JPEG images or on some well-known models in computer vision. EWNet and DBS2Net are gray-scale JPEG steganalyzers. Here, following the setting in reference \cite{Wei:22}, we adjust the number of input channels in the first convolution layer to 3, thereby enabling them to detect color images.

