\section{Experiment}

\subsection{Experimental Settings}

\paragraph{Datasets.} We employ three image datasets in our implementation, including COCO \cite{Lin:14}, ImageNet \cite{Russakovsky:15}, which are widely used in various deep learning tasks, and a standard steganographic dataset BOSSBase \cite{Bas:11}. We utilize 5000, 1000, and 2000 cover-secret pairs from COCO for training, validation, and testing, respectively. 2000 cover-secret pairs from ImageNet and BOSSBase, respectively, are used only for testing. Additional 10000 cover-stego pairs, randomly mixed from three datasets, are used for training the steganalyzer. The stego images are generated by HRJS \cite{Yanga:23}, a bit-level JPEG steganographic method based on deep learning. All images are center-cropped to $128\times128$. The compression quality factor adopted by the cover is 75, the most commonly used in JPEG steganography.

\paragraph{Baselines.} We take several advanced and representative methods as our baselines, including ISN \cite{Lu:21}, a DH-Spatial method, RIIS \cite{Xu:22}, a robust DH-Spatial method, and EFDR \cite{Yangb:23}, which is currently the only customized deep hiding method with color JPEG images as covers. Note that during the training phase of ISN, we add an additional JPEG compression distortion layer, referred to as ISN+ for distinction.

\paragraph{Evaluation metrics.} To measure the secret restorability and visual imperceptibility, we adopt peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR), structural similarity index measure (SSIM) \cite{Wang:04}, average pixel discrepancy (APD), and learned perceptual image patch similarity (LPIPS) \cite{Zhanga:18}. PSNR/SSIM with larger values and APD/LPIPS with smaller values indicate higher image quality. For steganalysis imperceptibility, we utilize the detection accuracy rate (ACC) as the metric. The ACC is closer to 50\%, i.e., random guess, indicating superior performance. ACC\_U, ACC\_E, and ACC\_D denote the detection accuracy rate of UCNet, EWNet, and DBS2Net, respectively.

\paragraph{Implementation details.} Before training MRAG, the surrogate steganalyzer is pre-trained 50 epochs on 5000 mixed cover-stego pairs with the parameters following the default settings. Subsequently, the pre-trained surrogate steganalyzer’s weights are frozen, and we train MRAG for 200 epochs on 5000 cover-secret pairs from COCO. We adopt the Adam optimizer with $betas=(0.5, 0.999)$, $eps=1e^{-6}$, and $weight\_decay=5e^{-4}$, and set the learning rate to $5e^{-4}$. The mini-batch size is 4, containing two randomly selected cover-secret pairs. Note that the steganalyzer used for the steganalysis imperceptibility evaluation is trained on another 5000 mixed cover-stego pairs. All experiments are implemented with PyTorch and on the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090 GPU.

\subsection{Comparison Results}

In this subsection, to verify the performance of MRAG in terms of secret restorability, visual imperceptibility, and steganalysis imperceptibility, we conduct extensive experiments on three datasets and three open-source JPEG steganalyzers.

\paragraph{Secret restorability.} Table \ref{tab:SR} reports the numerical comparisons of our MRAG with existing baselines. It can be observed that MRAG significantly outperforms these baselines in terms of secret restorability. Compared with EFDR, MRAG can supply at least 2.95 dB PSNR advancement. Similar performance improvements can also be observed in SSIM, APD, and LPIPS. Visualization comparisons are shown in Fig. \ref{fig3}. We can observe that the secret images revealed by MRAG are visually pleasing with minimal visual artifacts. In contrast, the DH-Spatial methods ISN+ and RIIS yield visible color distortion, blocking, blurring, and banding artifacts in the revealed secret images. It is noteworthy that although our model only uses the COCO for training, it still provides excellent results on the other two datasets, which indicates that our model has a favorable generalization ability.

\paragraph{Visual imperceptibility.} Table \ref{tab:VI} and Fig. \ref{fig4} report the numerical comparisons and visualization comparisons of our MRAG with ISN+, RIIS, and EFDR in terms of visual imperceptibility. We can observe that MRAG and EFDR have comparable performance, while the stego images generated by ISN+ and RIIS still contain many visible artifacts, making it difficult for them to achieve visual imperceptibility.

\paragraph{Steganalysis imperceptibility.} Steganalysis imperceptibility, an essential metric, evaluates the statistical imperceptible to resist steganalyzers. It achieves classification by distinguishing the changes in statistical features caused by hiding. Table \ref{tab:SI} shows the numerical comparisons of our MRAG with EFDR. It can be observed that MRAG significantly outperforms EFDR in terms of steganalysis imperceptibility. Compared to EFDR, MRAG can provide at least 15.2\% improvement, with most classification results approaching 50\%. Moreover, we visualize the distribution of the classified features learned by three steganalyzers using t-distributed stochastic neighbor embedding (t-SNE) in Fig. \ref{fig:tnse}. We can find that the distribution of covers' classified features (green points) and stegos' classified features (red points) obtained by EFDR can be well distinguished, while the distribution of the classified features obtained by the proposed MRAG is intermingled and difficult to distinguish. This also confirms the superior steganalysis imperceptibility of MRAG.

\subsection{Ablation Study}

Here, we mainly discuss the impact of the number of the affine coupling layer and multi-range branches.

\paragraph{Impact of the number of the affine coupling layer.} For the convenience of discussion, we fix the number of the affine coupling layer in the invertible local-range branch and the invertible global-range branch to 1, and focus on investigating the impact of the number of the affine coupling layer in the adaptive fusion module on performance. As reported in Table \ref{tab:ab_BN}, we can observe that the steganalysis imperceptibility performance, the most concerned metric, is optimal when the number of layers is 3. Thus, in our implementation, the number of the affine coupling layer in the adaptive fusion module defaults to 3 unless otherwise specified.

\paragraph{Effectiveness of multi-range branches.} Also, we verify the performance effects of the invertible local-range branch and the invertible global-range branch, as shown in Table \ref{tab:ab_LG}. It can be observed that compared to any single-range branch, our designed multi-range branches can significantly boost the performance of secret restorability and steganalysis imperceptibility while keeping visual imperceptibility.

\section{Conclusion}

In this work, we attempt to introduce generation-based adversarial attacks into color JPEG image deep hiding, exploring the potential of generated color JPEG stego images in terms of secret restorability, visual imperceptibility, and steganalysis imperceptibility. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first attempt to focus on the steganalysis imperceptibility issue of color JPEG image deep hiding. A multi-range representations-driven adversarial stego generation framework, MRAG, is proposed from a steganalysis perspective. Besides, a features angle-norm disentanglement loss is designed to constrain the generated stegos closer to covers in the angle and norm space of the steganalyzer’s classified features. Comprehensive experiments verify the effectiveness of MRAG and its superior performance. We believe the success of MRAG can further promote the development of color JPEG image deep hiding and provide some inspiration for further research on steganalysis imperceptibility.


